\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar/yen eases as BOJ fears ebb. \b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1999\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 11 (Reuters) - The dollar eased against the yen early in Europe on Friday, the protection offered by wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fading as Asian trading drew to a close. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro rose to its highest this month against the dollar, with traders trimming short positions in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid speculation that it may have bottomed out for now. \par \par \uc2  "In the next few (trading) days we are likely to see a situation where dollar/yen stabilises in Asia and late in New York because of worries about the Bank of Japan but drifts down in Europe," said a senior trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Dealers had shied away from pushing the dollar too close to 118.50 yen overnight amid concern the Bank of Japan would step in to halt the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, as it did yesterday. \par \par \uc2  While European dealers were aware that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have left orders to sell yen with other central banks, they were more inclined to test the dollar's downside against the yen and drove it down to 118.60/70 by 0748 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Job creation measures unveiled overnight by Japan, a rise in Japanese government bond yields, and a rally in the the Nikkei were all cited as yen bullish factors, particularly given the stronger-than-expected Japanese growth data released Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Eugen Keller, strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt, said the dollar was also being undermined by concern about the reaction U.S. assets could have to the producer price and retail sales data for May which are out at 1230 GMT today. \par \par \uc2  "People are getting worried ahead of the PPI release given the risk that it could hit U.S. stocks and bonds," he said. \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Industrial Average closed 0.65 percent lower on Thursday while U.S. Treasuries fell overnight, driving the yield on the benchmark 30-year bond to 6.08 percent from 6.06 percent in New York on Thursday. An extension of these losses will fan concerns that investors quitting U.S. assets will sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  The euro was best placed to profit from traders' inclination to sell dollars and rose as high as $1.0535 by 0803 GMT. The break above the $1.0520 level which curtailed its rally on Thursday was taken as another signal that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not about to revisit life lows near $1.0250 anytime soon. \par \par \uc2  "People are getting bullish overtures from the technicals and are speculating that we have now seen the lows for the euro," the trader at the Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  It will, however, take a decisive move above the crucial $1.0550 chart barrier to convince traders that the euro is about to embark on an extended rebound, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  While the euro was back at $1.0510/15 by 0816 GMT, it was still stronger than $1.0483/87 late in Europe on Thursday. Meanwhile dollar/yen was at 118.64/69, mired near session lows but still well above the seven-week lows near 117.60 hit on Thursday before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Sterling was also in the spotlight on Friday after rallying to a one-week high of $1.6133 early in European trading. \par \par \uc2  The pound's resilience in the face of Thursday's unexpected quarter-percentage point cut in the British interest rate was partly explained by expectations that growth would benefit from the move, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  While U.S. data is likely to preoccupy the market later in the session, traders said new direction could also come from a speech European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Tommaso Padoa-Schioppa is to deliver at 1215 GMT. \par \par \uc2  ((Swaha Pattanaik, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6b06m3r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
