\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar/yen loses ground, eyes 118. \b0\par\par\uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1999\par 13:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 11 (Reuters) - The dollar broke out to the downside from its tight morning ranges against the yen by the European midsession on Friday as the breach of a chart support triggered a flurry of sell orders, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step in again after its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Thursday was keeping the dollar above further support at 118, they added. \par \par \uc2  "We've fallen through 118.50 (in dollar/yen) and this has triggered some stop-loss orders," said a trader at a UK bank. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market ignored Japan's European Planning Agency minister Taichi Sakaiya, who said the market had reacted eccessively on Thursday to strong GDP data. \par \par \uc2  "Sakaiya is trying to talk the dollar up a bit, but the market is paying little attention to it," said a trader at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The Japan economy has been so bad for so long that as soon as market players saw a sign of growth, they were quite happy to buy yen," said a trader at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro retreated from its June peaks hit early in the session against the dollar after failing to tackle chart resistance at $1.0550 and was bobbing around the $1.05 level. \par \par \uc2  Euro-supportive comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chief economist Otmar Issing and International Monetary Fund first deputy managing director Stanley Fisher also provided little fresh impetus for this week's two-and-a-half cent rally in euro/dollar. \par \par \uc2  By 1100 GMT, dollar/yen was quoted at 118.36/41 yen against 118.72/77 yen late in Europe on Thursday. The dollar stood 0.7 percent above a seven-week low of 117.63 yen hit before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the markets on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro firmed to $1.0498/03 against $1.0483/87 late on Thursday but off a peak of $1.0535, its top in June. \par \par \uc2  Issing said on Friday the euro had potential to rise and that central banks should never say they would never \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Fischer said he noted positive signs of a pick-up in European economic growth. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said it was still early to say that the economy of the 11-nation euro zone had definitely turned for the better. \par \par \uc2  "There are signs of economic improvement, like the German quarterly GDP data earlier this week," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. "But the market still retains a fairly cautious outlook." \par \par \uc2  Foley said the next German IFO survey would to be crucial to assess the health of the biggest euro zone economy, as it was a good lead indicator for economic activity in Germany. \par \par \uc2  For the time being analysts said that the market was looking for a cue from the reaction of U.S. assets to May producer price and retail sales data, due out at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The key focus for the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for today and next week is very much the developments in the U.S.," said Jeremy Hawkins, chief economic adviser at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said there was a chance the dollar could remain flat as the data would not add much to U.S. rate expectations. \par \par \uc2  "If the data today is strong it won't unduly affect the dollar because people are already ready for a Fed rate hike," said Michael Metcalfe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at NatWest Global Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  A meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven nations this weekend was also in focus. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was firm but below an earlier $1.6133 one-week top. The pound proved resilient to Thursday's 0.25 percent rate cut as analysts expected UK growth to benefit from the move. \par \par \uc2  ((Lisa Jucca, London Capital Markets 0171 542 6745, elisabetta.jucca@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6b06jan\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
