\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen bulls grapple with BOJ fears. \b0\par\par\uc2 976 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 1999\par 17:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 11 (Reuters) - The dollar fell by one yen from its highs to test chart support at 118 yen in Europe on Friday, as fears gradually evaporated that the Bank of Japan would step in again to curb yen strength. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that although yen bulls were still feeling the bruises of Thursday's BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the dollar, positive Japanese economic data was encouraging yen buying in the short term. \par \par \uc2  "To be honest, people are not really worried about it (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) at the moment," said a U.S. bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that until the dollar fell well below the seven-week low of 117.63 yen hit on the back of strong Japanese GDP data on Thursday, the risk of renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was limited. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese officials are trying to hold dollar/yen somewhere above 117 yen," said Michael Rottmann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HypoVereinsbank in Munich. \par \par \uc2  He said that if the yen kept on strengthening beyond 117 per dollar, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would try to curb its advance by verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , using physical \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if the pressure mounted. \par \par \uc2  "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens very slowly then there is less chance that they (BOJ) will do something," said Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Lehman Brothers in London. "The speed of the move matters as much as the level." \par \par \uc2  By 1500 GMT, dollar/yen was quoted at 118.05/08 yen against highs of 119.06 and from 118.72/77 yen late in Europe on Thursday, but still 0.4 percent above its seven-week low hit before the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro firmed to $1.0500/06 against $1.0483/87 late on Thursday but off a Friday peak of $1.0535, its top in June. \par \par \uc2  The euro paused on Friday after rallying 2-1/2 cents this week, with little news to lift it towards the next key chart barrier at $1.0550. \par \par \uc2  Stronger-than-expected U.S. May retail sales on Friday reinforced expectations that the Federal Reserve will hike rates at the end of this month, but this failed to impact on the dollar as analysts said the market was already pricing in a rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Retail sales climbed 1.0 percent in May against economists' expectations for a 0.8 percent inrease. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets failed to respond to news that Russian and NATO peacekeeping forces were racing their way to the border of the ravaged Yugoslav province of Kosovo, concentrating instead on economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "No-one is really interested in Kosovo now," said Rottman. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the session the euro had also ignored upbeat comments by European central bankers and International Monetary Fund first deputy managing director Stanley Fischer. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chief economist Otmar Issing said on Friday the euro had the potential to rise and that central banks should never say they would never \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Fischer said he noted positive signs of a pick-up in European economic growth. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said it was still early to say that the economy of the 11-nation euro zone had definitely turned for the better. \par \par \uc2  "There are signs of economic improvement, like the German quarterly GDP data earlier this week," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. "But the market still retains a fairly cautious outlook. \par \par \uc2  A meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven nations this weekend was also in focus, although a senior German official said on Friday the ministers would not issue statements on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  Sterling came off an earlier $1.6133 one-week top, but was firm on the day and proved resilient to Thursday's rate cut, as analysts expected UK growth to benefit from the move. \par \par \uc2  The euro paused on Friday after rallying 2-1/2 cents this week, with little news to lift it towards the next key chart barrier at $1.0550. \par \par \uc2  Stronger-than-expected U.S. May retail sales on Friday reinforced expectations that the Federal Reserve will hike rates at the end of this month, but this failed to impact on the dollar as analysts said the market was already pricing in a rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Retail sales climbed 1.0 percent in May against economists' expectations for a 0.8 percent inrease. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets failed to respond to news that Russian and NATO peacekeeping forces were racing their way to the border of the ravaged Yugoslav province of Kosovo, concentrating instead on economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "No-one is really interested in Kosovo now," said Rottman. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the session the euro had also ignored upbeat comments by European central bankers and International Monetary Fund first deputy managing director Stanley Fischer. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's chief economist Otmar Issing said on Friday the euro had the potential to rise and that central banks should never say they would 