\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Bought $1 Bln -2: Effort To Stem Yen Appreciation \b0\par\par\uc2 208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1999\par 15:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Nikkei)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday afternoon, buying some $1 billion in its first move against yen appreciation since Jan. 12, The Nihon Keizai Shimbun Friday morning edition reported. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took action after yen buying accelerated on expectations of an economic recovery for Japan; that market sentiment came after an announcement of much greater-than-expected growth in gross domestic product in January-March. The BOJ and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  believe excessive appreciation of the yen could trigger adverse effects on stocks and stymie economic recovery, the sources explained. \par \par \uc2  Central bankers and finance ministers of the Group of Seven industrial nations agreed in April to back a weaker yen to prom Under MOF guidance, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday after the dollar dropped sharply to 117.50-118.00 yen. A series of dollar-buying moves served to further push the yen back down, leaving the dollar in a range of 119-120 yen. \par \par \uc2  Market observers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also aimed at preventing a fall in stock prices, on concern that yen appreciation could erode the earnings performance of exporters. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 10-06-99 \par \par \uc2  1340GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6a04gcn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
