\par\par \b \uc2  Japan reasserts authority over yen as G7 looms. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1999\par 14:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 10 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought the yen to heel on Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt a rally which was threatening to run out of control days before a meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven nations. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers had begun to bet that bands which have confined the yen for nearly four months were about crack after startlingly strong Japanese growth numbers boosted it to seven-week highs near 117.60 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  However, their hopes and yen gains were wiped out as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised the market with its first high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for five months. \par \par \uc2  This ensures \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  turns up at this weekend's G7 finance ministers meeting armed with the best of both worlds - a pick-up in economic activity and a range-bound \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today is definitely a pre-G7 move," said Nick Parsons, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Paribas Capital Market. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese have had enough of being beaten up at the G7 and are delivering what they have been asked. It looks as if they can go to this G7 and take holiday snaps." \par \par \uc2  Time and time again, G7 communiques have referred to the need to avoid excessive volatility in exchange rates and significant misalignments in the exchange rates of major economies and urged Japan to stimulate domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  Traders said it was therefore not surprising the Bank of Japan slammed the brakes on a rally which has already lifted the yen more than four percent against the dollar so far this week. \par \par \uc2  "It was probably the speed of the move which was of concern to the Japanese authorities, particularly given it has been clear they would like it to stabilise around 120," said Keith Lewis, manager, treasury markets at American Express Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Their credibility is definitely growing given how successful they have been in achiving this objective." \par \par \uc2  With Japan heading into the G7 meeting armed with data showing its economy expanded at an annualised 7.9 percent in the first quarter and an exchange rate which will not jeopardise growth, it will more likely be Europe than Japan which is in the spotlight at the G7, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  While Germany can point to the stronger-than-expected growth figures published earlier this week, few expect the euro's 13 percent decline against the dollar in the first five months of the year to pass without a mention. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chief Economist Otmar Issing put traders on their guard after he said on Thursday that maintaining price stability would lead to an appreciation of the euro and that this could come sooner than many had assumed. \par \par \uc2  "The question is, is he is trying to say they will address the situation before they sit down and talk about it at the weekend," said one London-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was less likely to try to manipulate the exchange rate of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than its Japanese counterpart. \par \par \uc2  They also warned that the experiences of 1995, when the Bank of Japan spent billions of dollars a month trying to stop the yen rising against the dollar, suggested it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed its climb. \par \par \uc2  "European \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has always been miniscule but the Japanese are aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interveners\b0 \highlight\uc2  and have been for many years so they will be prepared to spend more money if need be," said Adrian Schmidt, international economist at Chase Investment Bank in London. ((Swaha Pattanaik, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6a06m2q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
