\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Tietmeyer's comments invigorate euro. \b0\par\par\uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 1999\par 13:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 28 (Reuters) - The euro jumped to session highs against the dollar by the European midsession on Friday after Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer said he would not be happy with another decline in the euro. \par \par \uc2  The euro gained nearly half a cent after Tietmeyer's remarks, but traders were sceptical the gains could be sustained as the euro was still looking fragile, following its slippage to lifetime lows near $1.04 on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The euro has spiked up on Tietmeyer's comments," said a trader at a U.S. bank in London. "But it's likely that this rally will run out of steam." \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer echoed earlier remarks by future Bundesbank chief Ernst Welteke, who said he was concerned by the euro's drop. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer also said that markets should realise that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was doing a good job and see the potential behind the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  By 1030 GMT, the euro was quoted at $1.0495/97 from $1.0435/40 late in Europe on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The apparent indifference by European central bankers and officials to the 12.5 percent decline of the single unit since its launch has been one of the main factors in the euro's fall. \par \par \uc2  "Tietmeyer's comments provide the excuse for short-covering in euros ahead of the long weekend," said Kit Juckes, head of bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at NatWest Global Financial Markets. \par \par \uc2  Juckes said that a weak euro was desirable to revive the ailing European economy. He said that the latest remarks by European central bankers indicated that they were unhappy with the speed of the fall rather than with the softening in itself. \par \par \uc2  Chartists said that if the euro dipped below its last-ditch key prop at $1.0345, parity with the dollar would be in sight. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar rose against the yen, helped by a rebound from Thursday's four-and-a-half-month lows in euro/yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had shed more than two percent versus the yen late on Thursday, falling to a low of 119.60 yen. By 1040 GMT it had climbed back to 120.94/99 yen, as euro/yen rose to 126.90/10 yen from its lows just below 125 yen in Asian trading. \par \par \uc2  Talk of an imminent U.S. rate hike had sparked on Thursday the biggest point-drop fall in the Dow Jones Industrial Average this year, putting pressure on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The outlook for dollar/yen has changed to a more negative one over the last few days led by worries about the performance of U.S. stocks," said Robert Hayward, senior economist at BankAmerica. Traders said they would eye Wall Street's opening. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Ministry officials Haruhiko Kuroda and Eisuke Sakakibara said they found more \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability desirable, amid ongoing market speculation the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if dollar/yen fell further. \par \par \uc2  Hayward said he was ruling out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this stage as the dollar remained well in the middle of a 10-yen band it has held since mid-February. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was more likely to become a possibility if the dollar fell to 115 yen, he added. \par \par \uc2  The upturn in euro/dollar lifted sterling above $1.60. Traders said the pound was gearing up for a test of the $1.6055 barrier after having remained subdued all week. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999