\par\par \b \uc2  G-7 cen banks seen reluctant to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2 -analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Swaha Pattanaik \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 1999\par 14:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 20 (Reuters) - The world's major central banks look to be opting for words rather than cash to guide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates these days but reluctance to put money where their mouths are is seen by many as an invitation to buy more dollars, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  A rare insight into \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  thinking was provided on Wednesday by minutes of the Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee's May meeting which showed it considered high-profile \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to knock down a stubbornly strong pound. \par \par \uc2  Such a strategy was rejected for fear of failing and in order to keep the spotlight on its inflation-curbing mandate. \par \par \uc2  If the BOE's reasons are anything to go by, neither the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  nor the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt trends which have driven the dollar to seven-month highs against the yen on Thursday and the euro to lifetime lows last month. \par \par \uc2  "Central banks are much more cautious about the use of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than most market participants perceive," said Avinash Persaud, head of global research at State Street. \par \par \uc2  "They are afraid of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsuccessfully and making matters worse for themselves by losing credibility. We are also entering a world where the only policy lever left is the exchange rate." \par \par \uc2  Persaud expects the dollar to power on towards 128 yen, above the 124.79 high already hit on Thursday, and predicted the euro would suffer another bout of weakness which would drive it below recent lows of $1.0537 hit at the end of April. \par \par \uc2  Overt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Group of Seven richest nations (G7) was most recently seen in January when the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen scrambling beyond the 28-month high it was scaling against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The yen has since weakened by more than 13 percent. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen falling is expected to be less successful given that gloom about Japan's economy has yet to lift while U.S. growth has been strong enough to persuade the Federal Reserve to adopt a bias towards tighter monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities are therefore likely to shy away from throwing money at the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We seem to be in a market where the central banks are quite content to sit on the sidelines," said Juergen Lindemann, senior trader at IBJ in London. \par \par \uc2  "Everything points in the direction of a little bit more weakness in the yen, and while they may say something if the markets start moving in a faster gear, I would not expect any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  European policy makers are faced with a similar quandry with the euro, which has fallen as much as 11.5 percent against the dollar since its launch. \par \par \uc2  ECB officals, including President Wim Duisenberg leapt to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's defence when it sank towards $1.0550 in April, but there has been no indication that they are worried enough by its slide to spend some of their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  "The problem is that a weak euro is no bad thing given the economy is structurally weak. There is a limit to how low rates can go, and the ECB is arguing for fiscal policy not to be reflationary," said Persaud at State Street. \par \par \uc2  As for sterling, traders said there was little chance of a change of heart in the UK, where the monetary authorities have allowed the exchange rate to move freely since 1992 when large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to prevent the pound from being ejected from the European exchange-rate mechanism. \par \par \uc2  "I expect them to continue to talk about it but not to actually do it," said Jeffrey Woodruff, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of Boston in London. \par \par \uc2  The pound has risen by as much as 6.5 percent in trade weighted terms so far this year, despite several interest rate cuts and it shows no signs of weakening anytime soon, especially against the euro. \par \par \uc2  With British base rates a full 275 basis points above the ECB's current 2.50 percent rate, sterling is still seen as an attractive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold since it offers a much higher yield than the euro. \par \par \uc2  ((London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv5k00l8r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
