\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar drifts down towards 124 yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 1999\par 14:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, May 20 (Reuters) - The dollar edged down from earlier seven-month highs against the yen by the European midsession on Thursday, but remained resilient above 124 yen. \par \par \uc2  "There has been dollar strength again today, but it is coming off ever so slightly as people get cautious of pushing it too far too quickly," said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets in London. \par \par \uc2  Overnight comments from Japanese officals suggested the speed of any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves was of more concern than the current level of dollar/yen, rebuffing market talk of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Economic Planning Agency Minister Taichi Sakaiya said the yen's recent fall had been a bit rapid but saw no problem with dollar/yen above 124. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said the yen decline was not excessive and he expected no big changes in the dollar/yen exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry offical Eisuke Sakakibara, known as "Mr Yen" for his ability to move \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, was more cautious, saying he was monitoring the yen moves carefully. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 124.08/18 yen by 1103 GMT, down from the high of 124.79 and from 124.36/46 late in Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "There will be reluctance to move dollar/yen in a sharp manner, there is always the risk with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of having your fingers burnt and that has been the fear in the market in the last six weeks or so," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  She expected some consolidation in the 123.50 to 124.50 yen area for now, followed by a gradual trend higher towards 128 yen in three months. \par \par \uc2  "Compared with three months ago, there is disappointment with regard to how growth in Japan is panning out and that means medium term the yen can and will weaken further," she said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also supported by the limited reaction of U.S. Treasuries and stocks to the Federal Open Market Committee's move on Tuesday to a tightening bias. \par \par \uc2  "As long as the U.S. asset markets hold up, the carry trades will push the dollar up," said Bloom. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0642/47, from $1.0639/49 late in Europe on Wednesday. It came under pressure after the west German April Ifo business climate index fell to 89.7, from 90.2 in March. \par \par \uc2  "The dissapointing Ifo survey indicates Germany hasn't really benfited too much from the ECB's 50 basis point interest rate cut on April 8," said Foley. \par \par \uc2  "Structural problems with regard to the taxation and welfare systems and the labour market will continue to weigh on growth in core Europe and will remain a drag on the euro," she said. \par \par \uc2  Interest rates for the euro-11 were left unchanged on Thursday after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governing council's regular meeting monetary policy meeting. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said the market would also be watching the war in Yugoslavia for any signs of a peaceful resolution. \par \par \uc2  Sterling had recovered slightly from its two-week low of $1.6102 set earlier in Europe to $1.6134/44 by 1150 GMT. That compares with $1.6174/84 in late European trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The pound was at 65.93/98 pence per euro against 65.76/78 pence in late Europe on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said weaker-than-expected UK retail sales data had taken the shine off the pound. Sales fell 0.5 percent month-on-month and rose 1.6 percent from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  Traders are now focusing on the testimonies U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan and U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin are to deliver at 1400 GMT. Lawrence Summers, Rubin's deputy who will succeed him in July, also testifies. U.S. March trade data will be released at 1230 GMT. ((Deepthi Wickremasinghe, London Capital Markets, +44 171 542 4040, fax +44 171 542 5285, deepthi.wickremasinghe@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv5k00cw9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
