\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-BOJ dollar-buying offers dual policy pluses. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Risa Maeda \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1999\par 13:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 30 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan (BOJ) can kill two birds with one stone if it steps into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and buys dollars for yen, as such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would ease both \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credit conditions and the yen's exchange rate, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ is likely to have few objections to buying dollars and selling yen, which would complement its current monetary policy of pumping liquidity into the banking sector and holding down interest rates, said Koji Fukaya, chief analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division. \par \par \uc2  "If the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and leaves the banks holding an abundance of yen, that would fit well into the policy it set in February," he said. \par \par \uc2  On February 12, the BOJ's Policy Board decided to guide the key unsecured overnight call rate as low as possible, by supplying more ample funds to the money markets. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan drew a link between monetary and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy on Thursday, with comments indicating that attempts by central banks to influence their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value would be most effective if they were accompanied by complementary monetary policy steps. \par \par \uc2  Traders added that, with Japan's goals for both monetary and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy now pointing in the same direction, any dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to aim high. \par \par \uc2  "Once Japan takes action, they would try to boost the dollar to 130 yen or above," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Japanese bank, who asked not to be named. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was around 119.30 yen by late afternoon in Tokyo on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that recent comments by senior Japanese Finance Ministry officials suggested that, for now, Japan was simply aiming to avoid a fall in the dollar much below 117 yen, after exporters told the BOJ in a survey they would on average use a rate of about 116 yen per dollar to calculate their earnings for the fiscal year that began on April 1. \par \par \uc2  The ministry officials' recent warnings that Japan would take decisive action against a "premature, excessive rise" of the yen helped halt a recent slide in the dollar to around 117.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been under downward pressure against the yen due to recent capital flows into Japan, after foreign investors started buying Japanese stocks late last year on expectations that Japanese banks and corporations would finally take badly needed restructuring steps. \par \par \uc2  Japanese exporters are also consistent dollar sellers. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said Japanese monetary authorities are afraid the still fragile economy may slip back if a rise in the yen cuts off profits at Japanese exporters and reduces prices of imports. \par \par \uc2  The authorities actually checked such deflationary pressures by engineering a weaker yen earlier this year. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar tumbled to 108.21 yen in New York in January, the BOJ stepped in to buy dollars for yen the following day, dealers said. They believe that was the last time the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was followed by an unprecedented credit easing on February 12, amid worries that historically low prices for commodities and crude oil could have added to deflationary pressures in Japan. \par \par \uc2  "At that time, the BOJ was knocked down by the double-punch of a drastic fall in commodity prices and a strong yen," said Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Fukaya. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ is usually reluctant to consider \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates when setting monetary policy, but given the continued threat of deflation, the yen's rate is seen as a key factor affecting the economy. \par \par \uc2  On April 19, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said there were still risks that prices in Japan may fall further. \par \par \uc2  Regarding monetary policy, Hayami said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue its current "decisive" easy credit policy until concerns about deflation abated. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's next Policy Board meeting is on May 18. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-8570 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4u01ux4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
