\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Despite denials, markets expect new Japan budget. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Masayuki Kitano \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 1999\par 10:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 27 (Reuters) - Despite repeated government denials, the markets believe that Japan will soon have to take new steps to stimulate the economy and that is likely to help support the yen, traders say. \par \par \uc2  The view was reinforced on Tuesday by a meeting in Washington of finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrial nations, which took a stern view of Japan's economic performance. \par \par \uc2  Dealers and analysts said they expect the dollar to move within a range of about 117 yen to 123 yen over the next month. Any fall below the 117 mark will likely be short-term over fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and further easing of monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose against the yen overnight after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said on Monday that he told U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin that Japan did not need an extra budget for the current fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  It stood at 119.41/51 yen at mid-afternoon on Tuesday in Tokyo, compared with 119.05/15 yen in late New York on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether Rubin had suggested an extra programme of spending, Miyazawa answered: "No. They did not talk about that." But dealers and analysts said the language of the G7 communique suggested that there was strong pressure on Japan to take steps to restore growth, and it seemed increasingly likely that Japan would eventually be forced to compile an extra budget. \par \par \uc2  "Judging from the strong language in the G7 communique, I don't think Japan can avoid adopting a supplementary budget, regardless of Miyazawa's denials," said Takehiro Sato, assistant manager at Sumitomo Bank's treasury group. \par \par \uc2  "Adoption of an extra budget would spur yen buying not only because it would lead to a rise in JGB yields, but because it would also spur a rise in Tokyo stocks and stoke foreign investors' appetite for stocks," said Ryohei Muramatsu, manager for treasury and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  A statement from the G7 finance ministers and central bankers released after their Washington meeting said short-term prospects for the Japanese economy "remain uncertain". "It is therefore essential that Japan implement stimulus measures until growth is restored, using all available tools to support strong domestic-demand-led growth," they said. \par \par \uc2  Hiroyuki Onishi, director at Barclays Bank said: "A key for the market will be how and when the extra economic stimulus steps are announced." \par \par \uc2  The possibility of extra stimulus, along with a declining appetite for overseas assets among Japanese investors and Japan's large trade surplus, were likely to pressure the dollar, Onishi said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said speculation over a supplementary budget was likely to flare up ahead of Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi's meeting with U.S. President Bill Clinton on May 3, and a G7 summit in Cologne, Germany, in June. \par \par \uc2  "The market will be trying to guess what is being said behind the scenes," said Taisuke Tanaka, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  "After Obuchi's visit, the market will start to wonder if he has made any promises about an extra budget to Clinton," Tanaka said. \par \par \uc2  The impact of the speculation on the dollar may be mitigated, he said, if Japan's economy appears to be continuing its slide toward the end of the year, and if the BOJ embarks on a policy of quantitative monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  "If the BOJ adopts a quantitative easing policy, the dollar could rise toward 125 yen to 130 yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  According to the minutes of a BOJ policy board meeting on March 25 that were released on Tuesday, board member Nobuyuki Nakahara proposed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  target a 10 percent year-on-year rise in the monetary base. \par \par \uc2  Although the board voted 8 to 1 against Nakahara, analysts said the possibility of a quantitative monetary easing could not be ruled out. ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 5473 3748 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4r026k6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
