\par\par \b \uc2  Fresh G7 reference to yen unlikely-ex-officials. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kanta Watanabe \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1999\par 04:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 23 (Reuters) - Japan is likely to stress the need for a stable yen at Monday's meeting of Group of Seven (G7) nations, but chances of G7 making any fresh pronouncement on the yen are slim, former Japanese policy-makers say. \par \par \uc2  In a statement to be released after the Washington meeting, G7 finance ministers and central bankers probably will not go much beyond reaffirming their commitment to seek stable exchange rates among major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they said. \par \par \uc2  Kosuke Nakahira, a former vice finance minister for international affairs, said he does not believe there is any possibility of G7 targeting any particular level in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/yen rate is relatively stable. The euro is a bit weak, but there is currently no turbulence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as a whole," said Nakahira, vice chairman of the Institute for International Economic Studies. \par \par \uc2  "So in the G7 statement, I don't think there will be any reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is very different from previous statements," he said. \par \par \uc2  In its last statement, released in February in Bonn, the G7 said it will pursue policies "to help avoid excess volatility and significant misalignments of exchange rates of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ". \par \par \uc2  The G7 last made direct reference to the yen in April 1998, when it said it supported Japan's steps aimed at stimulating domestic demand and "correcting the excessive depreciation of the yen". \par \par \uc2  Japan could still act on its own to prevent further yen strength, as it would reduce chances of an economic recovery, former policy-makers said. \par \par \uc2  "Any abrupt swings in the exchange rate now could nip Japan's economic recovery in the bud," Nakahira said, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market if there are very sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa told a news conference on Friday that G7 nations would discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates but would neither approve nor disapprove of current levels. \par \par \uc2  "The G7 is not in a position to approve or disapprove the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate," Miyazawa said. "We will discuss the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue as we usually do, but whether the level is good will not become an issue." \par \par \uc2  Former Japanese officials said the present exchange rate of near 120 yen to the dollar would keep both Japan and the United States happy. \par \par \uc2  "I think both the United States and Japan would be content with the dollar/yen rate as long as it stayed within a range of roughly 115 yen to 125 yen," said Takeshi Ohta, a former executive director of the Bank of Japan (BOJ). \par \par \uc2  "So at the upcoming G7, there may be more discussions on the new single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the yen," said Ohta, currently a special adviser to the president of Daiwa Bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to a three-and-half week low of 117.43 yen last week before regaining ground this week on comments by senior officials of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF). \par \par \uc2  "A premature, excessive rise of the yen is not favourable and (MOF) will take decisive action to deal with such movements," Eisuke Sakakibara, vice finance minister for international affairs, said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  MOF comments and speculation that the G7 might agree to prevent further rises in the yen helped the dollar recover as high as 120.40 yen in Tokyo on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  According to the BOJ's quarterly "tankan" survey of business sentiment released earlier this month, major Japanese manufacturers assume, on average, a dollar/yen rate of 116.12 yen in business plans for the fiscal year that began on April 1. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers believe Japan's financial authorities would try to prevent a drop in the value of the dollar below that level, as that could force many Japanese firms to revise down their earnings outlook and trim their capital spending. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consultant Richard Medley told Reuters Television on Friday that MOF fears the yen strengthening above 117 to the dollar would rock \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stock and bond markets and is serious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the 115 yen level is threatened. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ is last believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the yen in mid-January, when the dollar fell below 110 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 119.35/43 yen in late morning Tokyo trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-9780 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4n02fsu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
