\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Euro/dollar capped by chart barrier. \b0\par\par\uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1999\par 13:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 23 (Reuters) - The euro's recovery in European trading on Friday from Thursday's lifetime lows against the dollar lost steam as it bumped into a stubborn chart barrier. \par \par \uc2  Even though a Yugoslav offer of a deal over Kosovo was rejected by the U.S. and Britain, dealers were reluctant to be too short euros in case of an improvement in the Kosovo conflict. The euro rose to $1.0683 from Thursday's $1.0558 low. \par \par \uc2  "Initially the euro was lifted by hopes of a positive announcement on Kosovo," said a trader at a European bank in London. "We then got up to a major resistance at $1.0680/85 and eased off as the market could not break it." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro was also bolstered by speculation that Group of Seven finance ministers might make a reference to the weakness of the euro at their meeting in Washington on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "They might say something to suggest the euro is too weak," said Chris Iggo, curreny strategist at Barclays Capital. "But the experience of the G7...suggests they are not going to come out with any big proclamations." Euro-positive statements by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Vice President Christian Noyer on Friday left the euro unfazed. Noyer said markets should not underestimate the euro as it was underpinned by a big balance of payments surplus. \par \par \uc2  Noyer added it was too soon to assess the impact of the war in Yugoslavia on the European economy. \par \par \uc2  The euro traded at $1.0632/37 by 1050 GMT from $1.0601/11 late in Europe on Thursday. Traders said the unit could drift lower, but should find a base between $1.0570 and $1.0610. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen the dollar was quoted 119.62/72 yen against 119.60/70 yen Europe late European trading on Thursday, up from a session trough of 119.20 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was underpinned against the yen ahead of the G7 meeting and by the perception that Japanese authorities would not welcome a yen rise as it would delay Japan's recovery. \par \par \uc2  Prominent U.S. consultant Richard Medley said on Friday Japan feared a rise through 117 yen per dollar would rock the nation's markets. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was serious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen rose above 115, Medley said. \par \par \uc2  "Japanese authorities would like to see a very stable dollar/yen rate," said the same European bank-trader. "Any move below 117 and BOJ would definitely be around," he said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was battling to regain a one-week top of 128 yen, reached on the back of the recent strength of the dollar against yen. Euro/yen was quoted 127.17/31 yen by 1100 GMT, 1.3 percent up from a three-and-a half week low of 124.79 on April 19. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar resumes a decisive upward trend against the yen, euro/yen might attack the 128 yen chart barrier, and possibly stretch to 128.80 yen," said Maurizio Milano, technical analyst at Banca Sella in Italy. \par \par \uc2  Sterling reacted with disappointment to UK preliminary quarterly GDP figures, the only key macroeconomic data scheduled for release on Friday. A quarterly increase of 0.1 percent in UK GDP came in line with Reuters consensus, but suggested that the British economy had not yet reached the end of the tunnel. \par \par \uc2  The pound lost nearly half a cent against the dollar after the data, but remained relatively stable against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc recovered from Thursday's eight-month trough of 1.5155 francs against the dollar to 1.5074/84, capitalising on the euro's bounce against the U.S. unit. \par \par \uc2  Swiss National Bank Chairman Hans Meyer said he expected the franc to remain stable against the euro for the rest of 1999. \par \par \uc2  The decision by the Norges Bank to cut key interest rates by half a point had little impact on the Norwegian crown, which was trading at 8.275 per euro at 1152 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar, meanwhile, slipped from overnight one-year peaks to trade at $0.6536/41. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4n02cis\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
