\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar steady, off lows vs yen in Tokyo, awaits G7. \b0\par\par\uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1999\par 09:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO April 23 (Reuters) - The dollar bounced from its lows against the yen by late Tokyo trade on Friday, after falling on long liquidation spurred by speculation Japan may unveil more stimulative measures to prop its ailing economy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But operators were reluctant to chase the greenback lower ahead of a Group of Seven (G7) meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors on Monday, they said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0632 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 119.50/55 yen, unchanged from late New York trade on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to a low of 119.18 yen in Tokyo morning trade after media reports, later denied, that Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi would announce more economic stimulus steps in Washington in early May. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar's losses were limited as an Obuchi spokesman said the prime minister had no plan to announce new stimulus steps when he meets U.S. President Bill Clinton on May 3. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa also reiterated on Friday he did not see any need for Japan to compile a supplementary budget for fiscal year 1999/00, which started on April 1. \par \par \uc2  Asked about a news report that the United States was likely to demand more economic stimulus steps from Japan at the G7 and U.S.-Japan summit, Miyazawa said: "The purpose of the meetings is to comment on each other's economy, but I don't think the United States will officially demand new steps from Japan." \par \par \uc2  On \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Miyazawa said he did not believe that any of the G7 nations were dissatisfied with current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  The greenback was also lifted against the yen in morning trade after U.S. consultant Richard Medley said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  was serious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the 115 yen level threatened to be reached. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Reuters Television, Medley said the MOF would prefer the yen at 120 to the dollar or even weaker, but that strong capital and current-account flows into Japan would block a significant yen weakening. \par \par \uc2  Trade thinned by Tokyo afternoon as dealers stayed on the sidelines ahead of the G7 meeting. \par \par \uc2  Dealers do not expect any specific references to the yen in the G7 communique as long as exchange levels do not deviate sharply from current levels, they said. \par \par \uc2  But they remained wary of hints that Japan may need to take further steps to boost the economy and raise investor confidence in Japanese assets. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said on Thursday "it is not a healthy situation" when only the United States is showing substantial growth. He urged other nations to breathe new life into their economies, warning there was "zero room for complacency". \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Commerce Secretary Robert Mallett said in Tokyo on Friday that Japan needs to take even stronger action "reasonably soon" in order to stimulate growth and generate a swift recovery. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a major city bank said: "First we will watch the G7 meet, then if Obuchi says something that brightens the prospects for Japan's economy. Such remarks could trigger further yen-buying by foreign accounts." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said selling interest by Japanese exporters above 120.50 yen would likely keep the greenback's upside limited while Japanese trust bank buying would support the dollar below 119 yen. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the euro recovered against the dollar on short-covering after Russian envoy Viktor Chernomyrdin was quoted as saying Yugoslav President Slobodan Milosevic was ready to accept an international presence in Kosovo. \par \par \uc2  "The euro should recover a bit in the short term if optimism grows for a breakthough in the Kosovo situation," the bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  But a dealer at a foreign bank said: "The impact of the Kosovo situation on the euro seems to be fading." \par \par \uc2  The euro's upside will likely be limited due to sluggish economic fundamentals in major euro zone nations such as Germany, the first bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at $1.0657/59 compared with $1.0642/47 in late New York on Thursday. ((Tamawa Kadoya, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4n02chh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
