\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup: Japan MOF Jawboming Lifts Dollar Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 1999\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Dollar-supportive comments by Japanese monetary authorities lifted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off of its early intraday low of Y117.66 and into the Y118 range Monday in Asia, leaving it above where it was at the end of last week in New York. \par \par \uc2  Both Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara and the ministry's international bureau chief Haruhiko Kuroda warned against excessive yen strengthening and said that Japanese monetary authorities would take action in such an event. Sakakibara even went so far as to say that he thinks that the U.S. shares Japan's concerns that a premature strengthening of the yen is undesirable for the country's economy. \par \par \uc2  "The comments from both MOF Sakakibara and Kuroda sparked dollar short-covering by some overseas players, in particular, U.S. funds," said a dealer at a Japanese trust bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also noted a speech by Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly branch managers' meeting Monday morning, but said that his remarks had no impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as they were in line with expectations. \par \par \uc2  Although the dollar, after rising on the finance ministry officials' comments, later tested strong resistance at Y118.50 as overseas players continued to buy during thin lunch-hour trading, it failed to break above the key level due to a lack of follow-through buying, said dealers. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar is now well supported above Y118.00 on fears of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  below this level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Market players are afraid of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities below Y118.00, as this is seen as the bottom of the MOF's desired range for dollar/yen," said the Japanese trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT (1:15 a.m. EDT), the dollar was quoted at Y118.23, above Y117.78 late Friday in New York but down from Y118.26 late Friday in London. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.0687, lower than $1.0701 late Friday in New York and below $1.0699 in London Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y126.35, up from Y126.03 in New York late Friday but below Y126.53 in London late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6132, down from $1.6136 in New York late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6132 in London late Friday. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 19-04-99 \par \par \uc2  0530GMT \par \par \uc2  1034 1863 28038 4162 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4j02x2j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
