\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar holds above 118 yen after MOF comments. \b0\par\par\uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 1999\par 09:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 19 (Reuters) - The dollar held above 118 yen in late Tokyo trade on Monday after bears scrambled to cover short positions on comments by senior Finance Ministry officials. \par \par \uc2  An early attempt to push the dollar below 117.50 yen failed after Japan's top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara warned that Japan would act decisively against premature, excessive rises in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also buoyed by remarks from Vice Finance Minister Koji Tanami, who played down comments on Friday by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa that the government may need to consider targeted tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 118.33/43 yen at 0625 GMT, compared with 117.78/83 yen late on Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  "A premature, excessive rise of the yen is not favourable and (the ministry) will take decisive action to deal with such movements," Sakakibara told reporters on Monday. \par \par \uc2  He also said the United States shared the view that an excessive yen rise is not good for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Although Monday's verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been anticipated somewhat after the dollar slipped to 117.43 yen on Friday, it was enough to stop bank dealers and exporters from selling dollars, traders said. Exporter selling was seen early above 118 yen. \par \par \uc2  "We were relieved to hear him (Sakakibara) speak at a level around 117.50 yen. Without his comments, the dollar could have easily dropped below 117 yen," said a city bank trader. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar may stay in an upward bias at least for today," he said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar extended its gains around midday on Tanami's comments. \par \par \uc2  Asked about Miyazawa's remarks, Tanami said he had seen news reports on the minister's comments, but added: "I think he sent a message while keeping his mind on economic conditions. But I have not seen or heard of the context (of his remarks) in detail." \par \par \uc2  Traders said solid bids were detected below 117.50 yen by Japanese pension funds on Monday, providing dollar support. \par \par \uc2  But some said a clear break below support at 117 yen would result in a quick fall to 115 yen, adding that such a move is highly likely as the yen gains momentum in cross trade. \par \par \uc2  "It is not only dollar/yen. The yen has been on an uptrend in crosses. That means the dollar's topside is firmly capped," said a trader at a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  A foreign bank dealer said nervousness over the Kosovo conflict had been encouraging a shift in investment from the euro to the yen and and the Australian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said actual dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities would likely be needed to prevent the yen strengthening to the 115 level. \par \par \uc2  "Further verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to have little impact," said the trust bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 126.01/12 yen at 0730 GMT, versus 126.13 yen in New York on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar fell as low as 117.55 yen, indirectly weighed down by euro selling against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The euro dropped as low as 125.52 yen earlier on Monday before buying by Japanese investors helped reverse its direction. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (ECB) seemingly tolerant stance on the euro's weakness could weigh on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by easing concerns over possible euro-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro hit a record low of $1.0628 in Tokyo on Friday. \par \par \uc2  ECB President Wim Duisenberg said on Friday he was not worried about the euro's fall. "The latest level I heard was $1.0680, and that's no cause for concern," Duisenberg said. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer said on Saturday he did not see the euro sliding below the one dollar level. \par \par \uc2  The euro stood at $1.0673/75 at 0740 GMT, compared with $1.0702/12 in late New York on Friday. ((Risa Maeda, Tokyo Treasury Desk, +81-3-3432-8570 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv4j02s84\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
