\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  View: Euro Expected To See New Record Lows \b0\par\par\uc2  By Barbara Etzel \par \uc2 974 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 1999\par 00:00\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro may be battered to fresh all-time lows against the dollar this week, while the Japanese yen will likely remain range-bound, with potential for some strengthening, said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and analysts. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday in New York, the dollar was trading at Y117.78, down from Y119.00 late Thursday. The euro was trading at $1.0701 from $1.0705 a day earlier. The euro sank to its lowest level ever at $1.0627 early in global trading Friday, but later recovered to finish only slightly down on the day. \par \par \uc2  The conflict in Kosovo and continued poor economic conditions are expected to continue pressuring the euro and keep many in the market bearish about the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Meanwhile, the yen could benefit as optimism grows about Japan's chances for economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Japan hasn't necessarily turned the corner but there are signals that they are starting to bottom out," said Robert Katz, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at MTB Bank. \par \par \uc2  While he is still bullish on dollar/yen, he expects the pair to remain in a range between Y116.80 and Y120.20 this week. At the upper end of the range, sellers will enter the market. While dollar/yen may test Y117.00 on the downside, rumors or concerns about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely keep losses limited, said Katz. \par \par \uc2  The euro's prospects had brightened after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rate two weeks ago, but pessimism has begun to creep back into the market. \par \par \uc2  "As the (Balkan) war drags on, so will the pressure on the euro," said Margaret Kudarauskas, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  Reports of atrocities against civilians, U.S. military reserves being called for duty and burgeoning numbers of refuges continuing to spill into nearby countries are factors which will all weigh on the euro, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  A euro level around $1.0535 corresponds to the Deutsche mark's 1998 low against the dollar. A move to $1.0500 is a possibility unless the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  begins \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively, said Kudarauskas. \par \par \uc2  But before it hits that level, the euro may find support at $1.0630, which was just above the new low made in Friday's Asian trading, she said. \par \par \uc2  From a budgetary standpoint, the continuing conflict means that more funds will be needed from North Atlantic Treaty Organization members. With European budgets already strained, the conflict presents a budgetary problem in addition to its humanitarian and foreign relations issue. \par \par \uc2  "There will be moderate erosion of budgets across Europe because of Kosovo," said Kevin Harris, international economist at MCM \b \highlight22\uc2 CurrencyWatch\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York. \par \par \uc2  But European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg claims he isn't worried about the new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stating Friday the euro's level against the dollar is "not a matter of concern." \par \par \uc2  "Basically the level has been steady for the last three to four weeks at about the same level (of the synthetic euro) for most of last year," he said, after a meeting with European Union finance ministers that was dominated by talk about Yugoslavia. \par \par \uc2  European economic data will be monitored. On Tuesday the German IFO business survey will be released. Seven analysts surveyed by Dow Jones are expecting to see an increase following nine months of declines. The reading is expected to come in at 90.7 against February's 89.8 reading. \par \par \uc2  Traders will be watching developments in Japan to see if the government is able to extend the modest momentum that began last week. Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said officials were keeping open the option of tax cuts. \par \par \uc2  Miyazawa also said he didn't believe it was overly optimistic to think that this "long slide will stop." \par \par \uc2  Further optimism was garnered by reports that the Japanese government would begin efforts to promote foreign direct investment also helped sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Sources also point out that weakness in euro/yen has contributed to yen gains against the dollar. As the conflict in Kosovo continues and as the economic situation in Europe remains difficult, pressure on the euro could continue helping the yen. \par \par \uc2  And, the market can be expected to keenly monitor any information on capital flows from Japan following last week's announcement by five Japanese life insurance companies that they would be cutting in half their 1999 purchase of foreign bonds. \par \par \uc2  Throughout the week finance and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials will meet in Washington when the International Monetary Fund and World Bank hold their Spring meeting in Washington. Group of Seven (G-7) finance ministers meet the following week. \par \par \uc2  While numerous comments will emerge, analysts said that it is unlikely the meetings will yield much to move \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "We've definitely seen volatility in dollar/yen ease up a bit," said Robert Katz, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at MTB Bank. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues have been bigger issues at previous meetings and likely there won't be too much focus on this, he added. \par \par \uc2  Also in the U.S., several speeches are scheduled by Federal Reserve officials. Their comments will be watched closely to determine if the Fed is satisfied with its neutral stance, particularly in light of recent European interest rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  U.S. trade data will be out on Tuesday and analysts are expecting a deficit around $16.5 billion. While the record trade deficit is a long-term worry which may spell trouble for the dollar, for now it isn't much of a concern for the market. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There might be a knee-jerk reaction after the numbers come out on\par Tuesday, but katz doesn't think "it will have any major effect in the\par near-term."\par  - By Barbara Etzel 201-938-4425\par barbara.etzel@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  04-18-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1800EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1141 4137 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010826dv4i00pl9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
