\par\par \b \uc2  China Fails To Sound Alarm On Yen, Despite Export Slump \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Wonacott and Karby Leggett \par \uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 1999\par 12:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING (Dow Jones)--China's exports are taking a beating, but Beijing has refrained so far from lashing out at its favorite scapegoat: a weak Japanese yen. \par \par \uc2  Although the recent slide in the Japanese yen against the dollar has coincided with a sharp slump in Chinese exports, few analysts - and more significantly - policy makers are drawing any parallels between the developments. China watchers say Beijing is mostly preoccupied with reforming its domestic economy, and not ready to blame a weak Japanese yen for its export woes - or even suggest it could cause a review of its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  That hasn't always been the case in this sensitive and scarred relationship. Perceptions that Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was digging into China's foreign investment and exports drew angry public responses last year from Beijing. \par \par \uc2  "The weak yen has had kind of a visceral impact on the Chinese," said a western economist based in Beijing. But he added, "at these levels, there's not much to worry about." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose to its highest level against the yen in 11 weeks in Asian dealings Monday, after finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the Group of Seven nations in Bonn failed to make explicit statements to counter the yen's recent weakness. \par \par \uc2  Though economists are debating the benefits of the moves, some say Japan's recent moves to relax monetary policy and stimulate the economy could push the yen even lower, perhaps as far as Y200 per dollar over the next six months. At 1100 GMT, the dollar was around Y122.00. \par \par \uc2  In the past, China has reacted to such developments with ominous signals. Last year, top Chinese leaders urged Japan to hold its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady - in part to bolster Beijing's pledge not to devalue the yuan. When the yen continued to wobble, official Chinese newspapers warned a weaker Japanese unit could force China to devalue its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a move that could undermine other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region and set back Asia's slow economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Though officials deny any link, China's repeated warnings were followed in June 1998 by a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Treasury to prop up the yen. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 22-02-99 \par \par \uc2  1103GMT \par \par \uc2  1143 4206 62558 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv2m01eat\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
