\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends U.S. mixed as yen bounces off lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 1999\par 23:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 18 (Reuters) - The dollar broke above 120 yen for the first time in 10 weeks, but surrendered some ground in late trading as investors booked profits, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The euro ended near its session low, even after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held interest rates steady, as market players remained convinced that rates must come down in order to stimulate weak European economies. \par \par \uc2  "If they lowered rates, that's not necessarily bad for the economies or equity markets, so maybe the fact that they did nothing helped the dollar. Either way, the dollar looks better right now," said one dealer at a French bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar got as high as 120.17 yen in New York afternoon trading but later pulled back, closing at 119.72/82, but was off slightly from its open at 119.85/95, but up from Wednesday's close at 118.90/00. \par \par \uc2  The euro ended at $1.1189/94, down from $1.1232/41 at the open and $1.1250/55 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The euro sold off in early afternoon. Dealers cited a flurry of euro/yen sales, taking the cross to about 134 from its session high at 134.83. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the dollar to resume its rally against the yen in the near future, with 125 a potential target. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials continued to talk their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower overnight. A prominent ruling party politician, Yasuhisa Shiozaki, told Reuters Television that the United States and Japan would probably not object if the dollar rose to between 120 and 130 yen. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with Britain's Financial Times newspaper, Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, reiterated that the yen should weaken after Japan cut a key short-term interest rate last Friday. \par \par \uc2  Those comments show \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prop up the yen as it slumps, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Japan is basically giving us the green light to buy dollars. They want the dollar higher," said one dealer at a Wall Street investment bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro fell against the dollar and the yen after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  anounced it would not shift interest rates Thursday, despite recent pressure from European politicians to ease monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  One of those politicians, German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine, sat in on the ECB meeting. He declined to comment upon leaving the meeting, except to say that the atmosphere was very good. \par \par \uc2  A European interest rate cut would tend to weaken the euro by lowering returns on investments denominated in euros. Many analysts and traders expect the euro and the Euroland economies to weaken in any case. \par \par \uc2  "It's not just interest rates. The economic numbers out of Europe have not been kind to the euro. It's going down whether they cut rates or not," said John Hazelton, a trader at PNC Bank in Pittsburgh. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's rally against the euro helped pull it higher against the Swiss franc as well. The Canadian dollar firmed somewhat, bolstered by technical factors, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to 1.4288/98 Swiss francs from 1.4222/32 at the open and 1.4200/10 at Wednesday's close. The greenback was lower at Canadian $1.4855/65 from C$1.4910/20 at the open and C$1.4950/60 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The British pound dipped to $1.6345/55 from $1.6352/62 at the open. The Australian dollar rose to $0.6356/61 from $0.6341/46. \par \par \uc2  ((-Chris Stetkiewicz, 212-859-1664)). \par \par \uc2  (C)