\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Roundup -2: Exporter Selling Slowing Dollar Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 327 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 1999\par 06:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Although dealers said they expect the dollar to test its upside in coming days, they noted that any U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ascent will be tempered by Japanese exporter selling ahead of fiscal year-end book-closing. There is also market concern that a too-rapid weakening of the yen could aggravate chronic U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade friction, inviting verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the move. \par \par \uc2  Market participants will also be closely watching international reaction to recent steps by Japan to prop up its economy when financial authorities from the Group of Seven (G-7) nations gather in Bonn this weekend. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro's rise Wednesday on the back of the dollar's technical fall has heightened sentiment that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may consider reducing interest rates - despite recent assertions that interest rate cuts are not in the works. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's policy board will meet Thursday amid calls from politicians for interest rate cuts to spur euro-zone growth. German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine, who has been one of the most strident proponents for a cut, will attend the meeting in his role as current rotating president of the European Union's Council of Finance Ministers. \par \par \uc2  Some European economists have said Lafontaine's attendance reduces the possibility that the ECB will cut rates, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will want to avoid the appearance of having given in to political pressure. \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentally, some of the euro-zone countries have real economic problems. In light of this, the trend is still to sell" the euro, said a dealer at a Japanese city bank. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also quoted at Y132.87, down from Y132.92 in New York late Tuesday but above Y132.07 in London late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6385, up from $1.6342 in New York late Tuesday. Sterling was at $1.6317 in London late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 17-02-99 \par \par \uc2  0554GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv2h019in\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
