\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dlr/yen cheered by Japan yen comments. \b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 1999\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 16 (Reuters) - The dollar was on a firm footing against the yen on Tuesday, though off the eight-week high it reached overnight, after Japanese authorities said they would accept a weaker yen if it was for the right reasons. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara - known as "Mr. Yen" for the influence his views have on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - said on Tuesday the government would accept a weakening in the yen as a natural consequence of Friday's credit easing by the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Separately, BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said the yen's recent drop against the dollar was positive, given Japan's current economic conditions. \par \par \uc2  "All this has led to a bit of a turnaround in dollar/yen," said Kirit Shah, chief strategist at Sanwa Bank in London. "For the past few weeks it's been trying to push higher and now I think it will continue to do so to 118 (yen) or 121.50." \par \par \uc2  The dollar had shot up to 117.50 yen during the Asian session, up about two yen from its session low, though it had fallen back to 116.89/95 by 0830 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen's rise had in turn pulled the euro down against the dollar, driving the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its lowest since its launch in January. It had risen about a quarter-cent from its low of $1.1192 by European morning trade. \par \par \uc2  Developments in the Japanese government bond (JGB) market, a key engine for the yen for some time, also helped push the yen lower on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa announced that his ministry would cut its issuance of 10-year JGBs for March and make up for this by boosting its issues of two-year and six-year debt. \par \par \uc2  The news cheered up a bond market struggling with an oversupply of long-term issues, with the 10-year JGB yield dropping by 0.155 point to 1.985 percent. \par \par \uc2  "The authorities have decided that instead of just watching, they are going to do something to stop deflation," said Francis Breedon, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  "A fall in the yen is part of this process. And the U.S. has pretty much said it will be content to see a weaker yen if it is part of a reflation effort," Breedon said. \par \par \uc2  While the rest of the G7 would likely applaud what Japan has done, they were unlikely to offer any specific cooperation as this was an internal Japanese issue, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  They also said that the recent rally in dollar/yen - which has risen 8.6 percent over the past month after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in mid-January - did not necessarily mean a change in the long-term trend. \par \par \uc2  "We've had economic indicators showing that the worst is over for Japan, and I don't think there has been a change in the primary downward trend in JGBs, although there will be a recovery," said Shah at Sanwa. \par \par \uc2  "So while there will be a modest recovery in dollar/yen, it's still inside a very wide range within a bear trend." \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro was playing second fiddle on Tuesday but its tone was generally soft - except against the sharply dropping yen - due to the current slow growth in core euro-zone economies. Concerning the euro, the market's focus was on the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's council meeting on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  While the market generally does not expect the ECB to cut its key 3.00-percent repo interest rate this week, there is some concern because German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine is due to attend Thursday's ECB meeting. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, Lafontaine was quoted as telling Italy's la Repubblica newspaper in an interview that high interest rates were destroying jobs in Germany and elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, chizu.nomiyama@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv2g04dss\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
