\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of Japan Reportedly To Sell $2.63 Billion In Financing Bills \b0\par\par\uc2 402 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 1999\par 06:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of Japan has notified money-market participants that it will sell 300 billion yen ($2.63 billion) in financing bills Monday, Wednesday's Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported. It will be the first time the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold financing bills since November 1997. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry issues financing bills to raise short-term funds, including funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ministry sells all the bills directly to the Bank of Japan, as they carry lower rates than the market offers. The Bank of Japan then sells financing bills to market participants via repurchase agreements to drain funds from the market. \par \par \uc2  There are different theories on the reason for the move, the newspaper reported. \par \par \uc2  The first is that the Bank of Japan is simply trying to drain liquidity from the market on a day on which a surplus is expected. Another is that the bank is preparing for the auctioning of financing bills slated to start in April. \par \par \uc2  In the past, the Bank of Japan has purchased nearly all the financing bills sold by the Finance Ministry. This is seen as probably why the Bank of Japan decided to have participants bid for the first time on Monday's bill offering. Up to now, when the Bank of Japan sold financing bills it predetermined the selling price. \par \par \uc2  Still others say the Bank of Japan is preparing to take a "twist approach." In a twist \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells short-term debt securities in exchange for long-term bonds, as a means of capping long-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa caused a stir in financial markets recently when he suggested such an approach as one of the options available to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a jump in long-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan officials have simply shrugged off such speculation, however, saying the bill selling will be conducted alongside its present open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank Of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar Jan. 12, buying between $3 billion and $4 billion to prop up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As a result, the dollar rose from around 108.5 yen to 111 yen and later built on those gains. Ever since, the dollar has traded between 112 yen and 116 yen. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv2a01w3x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
