\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-BOJ can't solve Japan debt deluge. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Dolan \par \uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 1999\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 9 (Reuters) - Don't rule out some form of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep a lid on long-term interest rates, but tinkering at the edges will not solve its ballooning debt problem, London-based analysts said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  After years of attempting to pump-prime the sputtering Japanese economy with a series of fiscal stimuli, the consequent accumulation of debt is coming back to haunt Japan. A simple case of supply and demand means interest rates on Japanese government bonds are rising and will probably rise further in the long term, no matter what tricks domestic politicians force a reluctant Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to perform. \par \par \uc2  Although talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since kept a rein on bond yields, the yield on benchmark 10-year Japanese bonds climbed as high as 2.44 percent last week, the highest in over 18 months. \par \par \uc2  "They can put off the day of reckoning, but it's still coming," Bob Attridge, head of fixed-income at fund managers Old Mutual Asset Management. "The Japanese have a huge habit of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the distortions caused by market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are often the sources of many of their problems further out." \par \par \uc2  Japan, with forecast net new borrowing for 1999 in the region of $350 billion, is set to account for 90 percent of total net government borrowing in the world this year, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The political clamour at home is for monetary authorities to do something quick. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has subtly shifted the pressure onto the Bank of Japan. The Bank of Japan, meeting on Friday, says it is doing as much as it can already. \par \par \uc2  Investors expect something to be done but most feel average Japanese rates are on the rise regardless. \par \par \uc2  "It (talk of moves to underpin JGBs) will dent bearishness in the bond market but average yields are still likely to go up longer term," said Kirit Shah, chief strategist at Sanwa International. \par \par \uc2  None of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  possibilities appears palatable to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although whether it has the final say is debatable, analysts said. Much of the pressure on BOJ stems from the domestic political environment and internal battles for supremacy between monetary and fiscal authorities, they said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  strongly opposes the underwriting of new bonds, which would in effect be done by printing new money, and this is currently illegal under the BOJ law. The Bank also baulks at the idea of outright buying of JGBs and says that would have the same inflationary effects, while destroying fiscal discipline. \par \par \uc2  "The likelihood of monetisation at the BOJ meeting on Friday is 20-30 pct," said Tetsefumi Yamakawa, director of Japan economic research at Goldman Sachs, Japan. "Monetisation is the biggest wild card for the JGB and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets." \par \par \uc2  One alternative is the much-discussed "twist" \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is aimed at funding the buying of long-dated bonds by selling short-term paper - essentially getting long rates down at the expense of short rates. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan already approximates these actions via the securities repurchase market, analysts said, and if these actions were conducted outright they would merely store up trouble for the future. "They may get long-term yields down temporarily but it won't improve the situation because the same amount of debt will be outstanding - only the average maturity will be shorter and this will ultimately cause problems most countries wish to avoid," said Attridge at Old Mutual. \par \par \uc2  ((London newsroom +44 17 542 6762, fax +44 171 583 7239)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv2904xpc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
