\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends U.S. day lower amid spate of rumors. \b0\par\par\uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 1999\par 00:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 4 (Reuters) - The dollar fell across the board on Thursday, wiping out early-morning gains as traders reacted to a host of rumours, blamed for sending Treasuries down and adding new volatility to already nervous markets. \par \par \uc2  Most of the market rumours involved the yen and how Tokyo may try to pull its economy out of recession but strong gains for the Canadian and Australian dollars helped steal some of the limelight, dealers and strategists said. \par \par \uc2  At the end of trading, dollar/yen stood at 111.95/00, below the 113.25/35 open and the key 112 yen level. Euro/dollar closed at $1.1333/43 up from $1.1260/65. \par \par \uc2  The dollar made a firm start against the yen as long-term interest rates came off after having hit an 18-month high earlier this week on fears that Tokyo may try to finance its fiscal stimulus plans by flooding the market with bonds. \par \par \uc2  But rumours that Japan's Finance Ministry had been selling U.S. Treasury bonds and urged Japanese investors to do the same to before the financial year-end book closings on March 31 added more volatility to already nervous markets. \par \par \uc2  There had also been talk that the Bank of Japan may underwrite Japanese government bonds (JGBs) to curb rising yields. \par \par \uc2  There had been a some talk that the United States had supported such a plan. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  representative in New York denied talk that a suggestion had been made to repatriate funds to Japan before the end of that nation's fiscal year on March 31 and the U.S. Treasury said it had not proposed to Japan that the Bank of Japan directly underwrite government debt. \par \par \uc2  While the market waits for Tokyo's next step to pull the economy out of recession, dealers expect to see a wide 110 to 120 dollar/yen trading range. Traders fear \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  near 110 after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved near that level earlier. \par \par \uc2  "It was really a combination of about eight different things," said J.P. Morgan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Jim McCormick about what moved the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  These however played against two main themes - the idea that Japan may be preparing some sort of money supply expansion either by underwriting JGBs or inflation targeting and the idea of repatriating funds in time for year-end, he added. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, talk of rate cuts again dominated trading after the Bank of England and the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates and dealers said it was only a matter of time before the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  too nudges its interest rates lower. \par \par \uc2  Even though the ECB kept the region's main interest rate at 3 percent on Thursday, news of more weakness including a 2.8 percent year on year drop in Germany's industrial orders in December fuelled talk that the U.S. economy will continue to outpace Europe. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, rumours that the January U.S. jobs report would be extremely strong also gave the dollar some strength, helping drive the euro to another life-time low. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg shrugged off the dollar's recent gains and said there was not need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  now in the markets. There had been talk European central banks tried to hold tight ranges. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the Bank of England trimmed rates by a larger than expected half a point to 5.5 percent which delighted traders who argued this would allow the bank more time before easing again. Sterling rallied and closed the day at $1.6450 after $1.6323/33. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar also surged, breaking through a key technical level as traders said it was benefiting from a rise in precious metals and the fact that the global economic crisis appears to have hurt key economies less severely than initially expected. The dollar fell to C$1.4898/08 from C$1.5023/33. The Australian climbed to$0.6484/89 from $0.6425/35. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped to 1.4130/40 Swiss francs from 1.4220/30.. ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1638 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv240546v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
