\par\par \b \uc2  OPTIONS-Front-end yen vols well bid as spot sinks. \b0\par\par\uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 1999\par 20:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, Feb 4 (Reuters) - Shorter-dated dollar/yen options were well bid in U.S. trading as the spot dollar continued to retrace its late-January gains and dipped below 112.00 yen again, traders said. \par \par \uc2  While the dollar's selloff from overnight highs above 114 yen was fairly big, traders said the move was within a recently well-travelled area, and the gains in volatilities looked vulnerable should spot stabilise. \par \par \uc2  "Each volatility rally has been less sustained," Alan Revercomb, options strategist at I.D.E.A. said of upticks in front-end volatilities this week. \par \par \uc2  "This recent one...doesn't seem to have much strength or staying ability," he noted. "You're going to need increasingly lower spot levels, increasingly surprising spot levels." \par \par \uc2  Shorter-dated volatilities, supported mainly by bid for the downside strikes, had been declining after spot bottomed out near 108 yen in mid-January and a long-inverted volatility curve was corrected. But the dollar's pullback this week has caused the curve to wiggle around slightly off lows. \par \par \uc2  Implied volatility for one-month options was up 0.3 point on the day at 17.7/18.0 percent in early afternoon U.S. as the dollar flirted with the 112-yen level. \par \par \uc2  One-month 25-delta yen calls slightly increased their premium over equivalent yen puts to 2.3/2.8 percentage points. \par \par \uc2  Traders said bleak Japanese economic fundamentals and anticipation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as spot approached 110 yen still supported the dollar's downside. \par \par \uc2  "If we do (make new lows), we're not going much beyond that. I don't think there's open-ended downside risk," he said. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar volatilities were little changed, in the middle of their daily range as the spot euro bounced to the mid-$1.13 area from early lows in the mid-$1.12s. \par \par \uc2  News the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not ease credit at its governing council meeting triggered a comeback in the spot euro, but traders said the downtrend could still continue. \par \par \uc2  "They've announced that the rate is unchanged, so there's going to be some short-covering," a Japanese bank trader said. "But then, at the same time, he (ECB President Wim Duisenberg) is making a comment that the weakness in euro is not a concern." \par \par \uc2  One-month euro volatility stood at around 8.4/8.7 percent, up marginally on the day. \par \par \uc2  Canadian dollar volatilities were bid as the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied two full cents against the U.S. dollar to new six-month highs in the C$1.49 area. One-month volatility rose a more than half a point to 8.2/8.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  The gains in option prices were relatively mild, considering the huge spot move, partly because the market's risk was perceived to be on the Canadian dollar's downside. \par \par \uc2  "If we had the 200-point (two-cent) run on the other side, one-month would be nine-something (percent) because the market is more skewed for a quick depreciation in the Canadian dollar," said a strategist at a Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  In the one-month area, Canada puts were priced about 0.4 point above calls. ((Chicago Derivatives Desk 312-408-8750 chicago.derivatives.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv240542j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
