\par\par \b \uc2  Yen's rise to be blunted by BOJ debt talk-analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tamawa Kadoya \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 1999\par 08:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 4 (Reuters) - Growing pressure on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to print money to help cover government debt and lingering risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely slow the yen's rise against the dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts and dealers say. \par \par \uc2  "The yen was strengthening on the rise in long-term interest rates and speculation of Japanese fund repatriation, but talk of the Bank of Japan directly underwriting JGBs may stem the tide in the short run," Kazuo Takayama, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at HSBC Midland, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  But analysts expect the dollar to retain a soft tone as dealers focus on shrinking interest rate differentials with the U.S. due to the rise in Japanese government bond yields (JGBs). \par \par \uc2  "Markets will focus on shrinking interest rate differentials until they confirm JGB yields have peaked. There are still downside risks that could spark the dollar's fall to or even below 108 yen," said Takehiro Sato, a senior analyst in the treasury group at Sumitomo Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Flows of Japanese money abroad will slow, while overseas money will flow to the short end of the rapidly steepening yield curve," Sato added. \par \par \uc2  A major Japanese newspaper reported on Thursday that U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin had urged the Japanese government to allow the BOJ to directly underwrite fresh government debt. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Treasury declined to comment on the report and Japan's top government spokesman Hiromu Nonaka said he had not heard that Rubin had made such a proposal. \par \par \uc2  An international financial source said comments made by Rubin on Wednesday were not intended as a specific endorsement of the idea. The yield on the benchmark 203rd 10-year cash bond fell more than 10 basis points by early afternoon, partly on the report. \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ did begin directly underwriting JGBs, effectively monetising or printing money to finance government debt, bond prices would normally be expected to rise and yields fall due to the extra demand. \par \par \uc2  But some in the market worry that such a move would undermine the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility and stoke inflation, which would have the opposite effect on prices and yields. \par \par \uc2  "Heightened talk about the BOJ monetising debt stems from views that Japan's economic situation is severe," said Ichiro Ikeda, director and head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts speculated that if Rubin had made such remarks, his motive could have been to prevent Japanese capital outflows from U.S. Treasuries. "Rubin's remarks may be attempting to counter Sakakibara's strategy for pushing for a gradually stronger yen and prompting Japanese investors to buy JGBs," Sumitomo's Sato said. \par \par \uc2  Japan's top financial diplomat, Eisuke Sakakibara, has recently made yen-bullish comments, saying that Japan's economy hit bottom in November and December and was now headed for recovery. \par \par \uc2  The yen traditionally strengthens in February because of seasonal fund repatriation and as Japanese investors refrain from allocating fresh funds ahead of March book-closings for the year. \par \par \uc2  But traders said rapid fund flows from foreign assets were unlikely unless the dollar made a sustained fall below 110 yen. \par \par \uc2  Analysts view 110 yen as a critical level where Japanese investors would begin dumping foreign securities in the face of heavy losses. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign assets are already showing unrealised losses but investors are willing to hold on to them at current dollar/yen levels...110 yen is a critical point," an analyst at a foreign brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  Fears that Japanese monetary authorities would attempt to stabilise the yen helped it bounce back from the 111 level on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  On January 12, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to check the Japanese unit's rise after it reached 108.21 yen against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was estimated to have bought around $6.7 billion to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in January, bankers said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  ((Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432 8027 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv24053hi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
