\par\par \b \uc2  Japanese Banking Leaders Reportedly At Odds Over  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Level \b0\par\par\uc2 190 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 1999\par 04:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- There's a rift among top officials at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  regarding how far the yen should be allowed to strengthen before authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the move, according to a report in a Japanese monthly. \par \par \uc2  The February edition of Sentaku quotes sources as saying Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara and Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa were at odds over the proper \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level during the run-up to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying action last month. \par \par \uc2  The sources said Sakakibara was willing to let the dollar fall to around 105 yen, while Miyazawa wanted to keep the dollar in the 110 to 120 yen range. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar Jan. 12, buying between $3 billion and $4 billion to prop up the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As a result, the dollar rose from around 108.5 yen to 111 yen and later built on those gains. Ever since, the dollar has traded between 112 yen and 116 yen. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv2401v1h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
