\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar up a bit vs yen in Tokyo but still bearish. \b0\par\par\uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 1999\par 08:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Feb 3 (Reuters) - The dollar recovered slightly against the yen by late Tokyo on Wednesday due to lingering concerns over possible Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but traders said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained vulnerable after its sharp fall. \par \par \uc2  The greenback carried over its bearish mood against the yen from overnight New York as early rounds of sales hammered it down to as low as 111.75 yen, but the market faced active short-covering and options-linked bids below 112 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The trend in dollar/yen still looks bearish, but many operators were not expecting it to drop below 110 yen because of strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns," a city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0640 GMT, the dollar stood at 112.33/43 yen compared with 112.12/22 yen in late New York on Tuesday. The euro was little changed at $1.1347/52 against $1.1345/50 in New York. \par \par \uc2  Rumours in the morning that the BOJ was checking rates as the dollar fell to its day low triggered short-covering, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Remarks by senior Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda on Wednesday morning that excessive yen strength or weakness was undesirable also helped the dollar up to a Tokyo high of 112.70 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said recent moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were partly caused by speculative moves in the market. \par \par \uc2  Asked if monetary authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves became excessive and what his views were on market expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kuroda said: "We cannot comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was becoming more sensitive about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ and such concerns were making operators reluctant to sell the dollar aggressively. \par \par \uc2  "You can see how nervous the market was about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only by looking at the way it reacted to the BOJ's rate check rumour this morning," a senior foreign bank dealer said. "In this mood, the dollar's falls could be limited." \par \par \uc2  The senior dealer said market concerns over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew stronger after Monday's foreign reserves data showed that BOJ dollar-buying in January was much larger than initial expectations. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Ministry said on Monday that its external reserves rose $6.262 billion from a month earlier to $222.211 billion at the end of January. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said overnight comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin also provided support to dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  Rubin reiterated on Tuesday that the administration's dollar policy remained unchanged. \par \par \uc2      "Our dollar policy remains absolutely unchanged. I believe \par \par \uc2   the stronger dollar has served our economy well over the last six \par \par \uc2   years and...we will not use our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an instrument of \par \par \uc2  trade policy," Rubin told reporters after an appearance before the Senate Finance Committee. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a recent uptrend in Japanese interest rates had helped induce yen buying, but such bids waned by late trade as Japanese government bond (JGB) yields were off the day's high. \par \par \uc2  At 0728 GMT, the benchmark 203rd 10-year JGB yield stood at 2.340 percent after hitting a morning high of 2.440 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said lingering speculation that many Japanese investors may be shifting part of their foreign bond assets back home due to the sharp rise in JGB yields has induced speculators to sell dollars against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "As long as speculation over repatriation by Japanese investors is there the dollar should stay under downward pressure against the yen," the city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the euro also remained under pressure against the yen. Many operators were waiting for the outcome of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy meeting on Thursday. The euro stood at 127.28/39 yen against 127.28 in late New York. ((Chikafumi Hodo, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv2305984\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
