\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr mixed at US open, mkt wary of BoJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 1999\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 3 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped marginally against the yen after a dip in Asia as Japanese interest rates continued to climb, but rumours of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are keeping the yen capped. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government continued to shrug off the rising benchmark 10-year JGB yield, which closed on Wednesday at 2.35 percent, after getting as high as 2.44 percent. The jump in yields helped push dollar/yen below 112 in early trade. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said dollar/yen buying by short-term speculators and some Japanese repatriation of capital could be applying pressure to the dollar versus the yen. \par \par \uc2  But fear that the BoJ might step in to cap the yen amid rumours that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was checking rates over Asia is keeping traders on their toes, fearful of pushing dollar/yen too low, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials also helped curb the yen's climb as they expressed doubts that long-term JGB yields would continue to rise and denied recent speculation that the government is following a policy of "benign neglect" with regard to rates. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen opened at 112.10/20, down from its Tuesday close of 112.12/22. Meanwhile, it rose marginally against the euro. Euro/dollar fell to $1.1339/44 from its $1.1345/50 close. \par \par \uc2  In early trade, dollar/yen held above support at 111.80. \par \par \uc2  "I think (the yen) has a limited downside," said Tom Benfer, director, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  "If we hit 110 or below, you've got to be wary (of BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ). A stronger yen is in no one's best interest," Benfer added. \par \par \uc2  Rising JGB yields, which jumped to an 18-month high on Tuesday, and the nonchalance of Japanese finance officials sparked calls by Japanese policians demanding the BoJ buy JGBs directly. Liberal Democratic Party member Ichizo Ohara announced he is considering such a proposal. \par \par \uc2  Ohara said Wednesday he was thinking about a proposal to have the BoJ personally underwrite at least five trillion yen in JGBs over two years. \par \par \uc2  For now, such calls fell on deaf ears. Japan Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa told Reuters rising interest rates were "nothing to get excited about." \par \par \uc2  But some market analysts remained concerned that the increasing cost of debt to the government could seriously hinder Japan's climb out of a prolonged recession. \par \par \uc2  "This is a threat for the Japanese economy," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics. \par \par \uc2  The laissez-faire attitude of Japanese finance officials shows "an absolute void of leadership on the economic front," he added. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro slipped slightly, but held its ground after a slide below $1.13 earlier in the week. Dealers said investors were squaring positions ahead of the two-day Federal Open Market Committee meeting slated to end Wednesday and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting Thursday. \par \par \uc2  There has been "a little bit of profit-taking on the short positions," Gilmore said, adding the market is discounting steady policy by both the Fed and the ECB. \par \par \uc2  Dealers were also awaiting the outcome of a two-day BoE monetary policy committee meeting. In a Reuters survey, economists said the MPC would hold rates steady in the near term, but would cut rates by 25-basis points three months out. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was softer Wednesday amid reports of limpness in the UK services and retail sectors. The British employers' group CBI said retail sales volumes fell in January and the sales outlook for February was the worst in four years. And the Chartered Institue of Purchasing and Supply said its Purchasing Managers' Index for services in January was 48.6, a rise from December's 47.4, but still proof of contraction. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv2305946\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
