\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Yen rally halted by fear of BOJ. \b0\par\par\uc2 600 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 1999\par 18:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Feb 3 (Reuters) - The yen's three-day rally against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was halted on Wednesday as fear of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  offset expectations of Japanese capital repatriation. \par \par \uc2  Expectations that rising Japanese bond yields will lure Japanese investors home have boosted the yen more than 3.5 percent against the dollar so far this week and lifted it 4.5 percent against the euro. \par \par \uc2  However, its rise ground to a halt as the dollar fell to within two yen of 110, a level viewed as the limit of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  tolerance following its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on January 12. \par \par \uc2  "The fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is putting a floor under dollar/yen while the fear of repatriation is limiting the upside," said Jane Foley, strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "These two forces will continue to dominate the market in the near term and keep dollar/yen in a 110 to 115 range." \par \par \uc2  The yen was 112.15/25 per dollar and 127.34/39 per euro at 1545 GMT from Wednesday's lows of 111.78 and 126.80. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that while yen bulls could pluck up the courage to challenge the Bank of Japan, they were likely to be perturbed by calls for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to finance government debt, which would effectively require it to print money. \par \par \uc2  "We will probably see a continuation of yen strength but if there is any change in stance by the BOJ, it would be the start of the turnaround and would make me bearish about the yen," said a trader at a continental European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Even a meeting of the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee could not shift the spotlight from the yen given that traders expect the target fed funds rate to be left unchanged at 4.75 percent when the two-day discussions end on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Stronger-than-expected U.S. growth and calmer international financial conditions have fanned speculation that U.S. rates are at or close to their lows for this economic cycle. \par \par \uc2  While signs of a slowdown in European economic activity have forced the euro to successive record lows in the past two weeks, it won a breathing space on Wednesday. It was trading at $1.1345/50 at 1634 GMT, compared with Tuesday's trough of $1.1280 and late European close of $1.1340/45. \par \par \uc2  The two-day meeting of the Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee, which began on Wednesday, generated more interest, with the consensus coming down on the side of a rate cut. \par \par \uc2  However, economists said it was likely to be a close call and that sterling was unlikely to lose much ground whatever the BOE's rate setting committee decided. \par \par \uc2  Foley at Barclays Capital predicted sterling would gain if rates were left unchanged and not fall far even if they were cut given such a move would nurture the economic turnaround seen in recent sentiment surveys. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.6384/89 from $1.6443/53 late in Europe on Tuesday. It was at 69.18/29 pence per euro, up from Tuesday's record high of 68.51 and close of 68.94/97. \par \par \uc2  Meantime, the New Zealand dollar climbed to nine-month peaks against the dollar with trading systems which use technical models fuelling the buying. Its gains were halted soon after the break of the psychological $0.55 barrier amid selling by New Zealand corporates, traders said. \par \par \uc2  ((Swaha Pattanaik, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)). \