\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar ends US down after stumble vs yen and euro. \b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 1999\par 23:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Feb 2 (Reuters) - The dollar tumbled over three yen on Tuesday to erase the last remains of gains it had packed on since the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-January to check the yen's surge against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell against the euro, paring some of its recent gains generated by expectations for ongoing strong U.S. economic growth as prospects in Europe wilt. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen fell to 112.12/22 from its open at 112.52/66, extending the overnight drop from Monday's 115.00/05 New York close. The pair however managed to crawl back above the critical 112 level after hitting a 111.95 session low. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar closed at $1.1345/50, up from $1.1334/39 at the open and $1.1296/11 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  After strong U.S. economic growth fuelled trading several days last week, traders virtually ignored the U.S. economy on Tuesday, turning their eyes toward rising Japanese interest rates instead. \par \par \uc2  The yield on Japan's benchmark 10-year bond hit an 18-month high of 2.31 percent amid worries the Tokyo government may try to fund its economic stimulus plans by flooding the market with bonds. This marks a sharp turnaround from yields of around 0.7 percent late last year. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa helped accelerate the drop in JGB prices by saying he would monitor developments but that "for now we should let the markets decide". Other ministers have said yields of around two percent were not high by global standards and. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been poised for a shake-up for about a week and there were stop losses near 112.80, once that level cracked the floor opened up for the dollar," said John Schein, vice president at Generale Bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers agreed the dollar may soon fall to 111.50, if the 112 level gives way. \par \par \uc2  Rumours the Bank of Japan may be preparing to buy dollars in the open market as it did on January 12 as well as comments from U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin that nothing had changed about the U.S. dollar policy, stemmed some losses. Traders were also relieved the dollar is not a trade tool. \par \par \uc2     "Our dollar policy remains absolutely unchanged. I believe \par \par \uc2   the strong dollar has served our economy well over the last six \par \par \uc2   years and ... we will not use our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an instrument of \par \par \uc2  trade policy," Rubin told reporters. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers feared trade frictions with Japan over cheap steel exports could prompt the Clinton administration to let the dollar drop and make U.S. goods less expensive abroad. "The Rubin comments didn't exactly suit the market," Ralph DelZenero, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at First Chicago said, adding however that some dealers may soon feel the time is right to buy dollars again. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar sailed to new highs against the euro for four consecutive sessions on expectations of lower interest rates in Europe, dealers said it was time to lock in some profits and slow the selling. Also a softer euro stemmed some of the talk for an early ECB rate cut. \par \par \uc2  "The story is still the same, strong growth in the United States and softer growth in Europe," DelZenero said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also breathed a sigh of relief as Brazil's real steadied even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  got its third president in as many weeks. \par \par \uc2  In other trading the British pound slipped to $1.6390/00 from $1.6450/60 while the dollar traded virtually unchanged against the Swiss franc at 1.4120/30 versus 1.4121/26. The dollar rose to Canadian $1.5142/52 from C$1.5058/68. The Australian dollar closed at $0.6370/75 down from $0.6383/88. ((-Svea Herbst-Bayliss, NA Treasury Desk, 212-859-1639 )). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv22057im\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
