\par\par \b \uc2  Lafontaine Favors Economic Alignment Over  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 1999\par 18:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BONN -(Dow Jones)- German Finance Minister Oskar Lafontaine on Wednesday said sustainable exchange-rate stability can only be achieved through economic alignment of different countries, not through \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Drawing back from more outspoken calls for target-zones between the world's major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Lafontaine said that "without real economic alignment, you can have as many target zones between the dollar and the yen as you want - they won't be of any use, you won't get anywhere with your target zones." \par \par \uc2  Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar against the yen is testament to this, he said. \par \par \uc2  "If somebody says we have to react with large \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (to stabilize exchange rates), then I refer only to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This can be avoided by coordinating real economic alignment," Lafontaine said at a press conference. \par \par \uc2  In its annual economic report published earlier Wednesday, the finance ministry said it "isn't aiming" for fixed trading bands between \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but stressed the need for real economic alignment to strengthen exchange rate stability. Despite confusing signals from the German government in recent months, it does seem that the Bonn administration is backing away from some of its more radical proposals. \par \par \uc2  While Lafontaine's boss, Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder, has always been skeptical about target zones, the finance minister caused controversy at the end of last year with his proposal for trading bands between the euro, dollar and yen. Later, he seemed to backpedal from this position only to come out in favor of it again two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  As part of the efforts to stabilize international exchange rates and financial systems, Lafontaine on Wednesday reiterated calls for more control of short-term capital movements and better banking regulations. \par \par \uc2  The necessity of these measures isn't disputed within the G-7, Lafontaine pointed out. "The full deregulation of short-term capital movement and exchange rates remains the dogma of fundamentalists." \par \par \uc2  On the economic report's projection of a stronger euro against the dollar over the course of the year, Lafontaine pointed to the U.S. balance of trade deficit, but refrained from "any further discussion on the topic of exchange rates." \par \par \uc2  -By Daniel Schwammenthal; 0049 228 215 7171 \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv1r01rt2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
