\par\par \b \uc2  Japan not facing deflationary tailspin-Sakakibara. \b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 1999\par 03:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 27 (Reuters) - Japan is not facing a "deflationary tailspin" and its authorities will seek stable exchange rates reflecting fundamentals, top financial diplomat Eisuke Sakakibara said in an commentary published Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  In a column in the Financial Times newspaper, the vice minister for international affairs also rejected assertions that Japan is not printing enough money to combat its financial woes and that government borrowing is "crowding out" that by the private sector. \par \par \uc2  "Although we are not optimistic that the real economy will turn around quickly because of the lingering impact of the credit crunch and the loss of confidence since late 1997, there is no reason to believe that Japan is about to go into a deflationary tailspin," Sakakibara said. \par \par \uc2  "The financial crisis is about to end and with some time lag the real sector will bounce back." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara, in the piece titled "Japan: don't panic", was specifically defending Tokyo against charges by critic Paul Krugman, a prominent economist at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. \par \par \uc2  Taking issue with Krugman's "simplistic argument that money should be created in whatever way possible", Sakakibara said the authorities have supplied abundant liquidity to the market since the "the panic of October-November 1997". \par \par \uc2  Despite reduced bank lending, 1998 monetary growth averaged 4.0 percent, up from 3.1 percent in 1997, he noted. \par \par \uc2  On crowding out, he said that although Japan's total government debt exceeds a full year's gross domestic product, "our net debt, including the social security fund, is less than 40 percent of GDP, the lowest of all Group of Seven countries." \par \par \uc2  Noting that 100 trillion yen of that is held by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Trust Fund Bureau and that Japanese personal financial assets are some 1,200 trillion yen, Sakakibara said: "There is absolutely no possibility of crowding out now or in the foreseeable future." \par \par \uc2  On the dangers posed to Japan's economy by a high yen, Sakakibara said that "the Japanese authorities have showed their determination to stop the yen's excessive strength". \par \par \uc2  Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya has confirmed that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets on January 12, selling yen when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit 108 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara did not comment directly on acceptable levels for the yen. \par \par \uc2  "What is really important for the Japanese economy is not necessarily a particular exchange rate but stability within a reasonable range," he said. "We intend to achieve this as long as stability is consistent with the fundamentals of Japan against the rest of the world." \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said that during the crisis of late 1997 and the first half of 1998, jittery banks "placed their short-term liquidity in JGBs (Japanese government bonds) and created what could be called a JGB bubble". \par \par \uc2  He noted that JGB prices declined after fears receded, but did not comment on present JGB prices or yields. ((Tokyo Newsroom +81-3 3432 8022 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1r05wmo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
