\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dlr/yen ranging, trade issue dominant. \b0\par\par\uc2 620 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Januar 1999\par 12:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 26 (Reuters) - The dollar was imprisoned in a half yen range above 113 in midsession trade on Tuesday but with a softer bias after trade tensions between the United States and Japan came back into the spotlight. \par \par \uc2  In overnight trade dollar/yen took a hit after U.S. Deputy Trade Representative Richard Fisher highlighted the build-up of hostility with his comments on Japanese exports, with particular reference to steel. \par \par \uc2  "Comments by senior U.S. officials on trade this afternoon will keep dollar sentiment negative," said Alistair MacMillan, an institutional salesman at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Trade Representative Charlene Barshefsky, Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin and Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers testify on trade initiatives before the Senate Finance Committee later on Tuesday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  "What they say will probably be the main focus for the dollar this afternoon," a trader said. "Brazil is a little quieter, but it could blow up again with a negative effect on the Dow and the dollar." \par \par \uc2  At 1116 GMT the dollar was at 113.43/47 yen against 113.70/75 in late Europe trade on Monday, the low of 113.31 has been tested several times during the session. \par \par \uc2  Brazil attempted to reassure nervous investors on Monday, saying it would not resort to capital controls by limiting the amount of dollars investors can take out of the country. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said this sort of comment helps, but Brazil needs to make serious progress on fiscal austerity measures and cut interest rates before confidence comes back. \par \par \uc2  "The problem with (last week's) devaluation of the real and the hike in interest rates is that it makes debt servicing excruciating painful," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  After floating the real early last week, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also raised official interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real opened trade at 1.81 per dollar from a 1.79 per dollar close on Monday, while interest rates were signalled at 32.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  Fitch IBCA rating agency cut its long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating for Brazil on Tuesday to B from B plus saying the devaluation and subsequent floating of the real complicated an already demanding external and domestic financing position. \par \par \uc2  The other factor working against dollar/yen is the repatriation of Japanese funds to domestic markets, said Andrew Milligan, economic adviser at CGU Asset Management. "We may see 110 again before the end of the Japanese fiscal year." \par \par \uc2  "But dollar/yen downside is hampered by fears of dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Milligan said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month, an attempt by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders to push dollar/yen down resulted in apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which effectively set a floor at 110. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said optimism the Japanese government may support bank sector shares helped the Nikkei to close up 1.22 percent and provided another prop for the yen. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar was also stuck in a tight range between $1.1576 and $1.1553. AT 1138 GMT the euro was at $1.1568/73, little changed from late Monday's $1.1570/76. \par \par \uc2  "The euro does appear to be acquiring a softer tone," Milligan said. "A lot of it to is to do with weakness in European economic growth." \par \par \uc2  Expectations of lower Euro interest rates sooner rather than later are working in concert with growing perceptions the U.S. economy does not need lower interest rates yet, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The steady U.S. interest rate scenario was reinforced by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan last week when he said the economy of the United States was "sparkling". \par \par \uc2  ((Pratima Desai, London newsroom +44 171 542 2737, pratima.desai@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1q05u6j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
