\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dlr confined, Brazil seen as pivotal. \b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 1999\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 25 (Reuters) - The dollar continued to cling to narrow ranges in midsession Europe on Monday, while attention remained focused on Brazil and other emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  The Monday session started off with talk of a Chinese yuan devaluation which gave the dollar some support versus the yen, said Teis Knuthsen, chief strategist at SE Banken. \par \par \uc2  A Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman later dismissed the newpaper report of a yuan devaluation, saying it did not reflect the official view. \par \par \uc2  "The focus this week will be on what is going to happen in Brazil and what the authorities are going to do," Knuthsen said. \par \par \uc2  "It's not over yet. After floating the real, Brazil has made all the wrong decisions." \par \par \uc2  Analysts agree the decision to raise Brazilian interest rates by nearly 20 percent last Monday was wrong, as was the decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to support the real. \par \par \uc2  "They (Brazil) should have cut rates, if only by a nominal amount. This would have stopped the capital outflows as people started to expect further cuts and some people may have decided it was the right time to go back into Brazil," Knuthsen said. \par \par \uc2  "We don't know what they will do. They may even invoke the ultimate escape plan - called capital controls." \par \par \uc2  A partial market holiday in Brazil on Monday may, however, continue to subdue activity, traders said. \par \par \uc2  By 1107 GMT the dollar was at 114.40/50 yen, in the middle of a session range between 114.80 yen and 114.19 yen. Late Friday it stood at 114.65/75. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at $1.1585/88, little changed from Friday's $1.1585/90. During the Monday session it has ranged between $1.1610 and $1.1575. \par \par \uc2  Peter Stoneham, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets said the markets are also trying to see which area the contagion may spread to next. \par \par \uc2  "They will be hunting for the next weak link, in terms of the knock-on effect," Stoneham said. "Brazilian problems have leapfrogged into Asia." \par \par \uc2  Many analysts believe the next \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come under pressure could be those based on commodities, such as the Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars. \par \par \uc2  The turmoil and the impact on world economic growth hits commodity demand and in turn the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of major commodity exporters, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "However dollar/yen is finely balanced between 112 and 118. There is decent liquidity and depth in the dollar/yen market." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said continued Japanese repatriation of funds from Europe and the United States would continue to support the yen, while lingering fears of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cap yen gains at 110. \par \par \uc2  "Higher yields on Japanese government bonds are giving credence to the idea of repatriation," said Stoneham. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, strategists say the narrow ranges the dollar has held are partly as a result of low liquidity. \par \par \uc2  "Euro/dollar is still a non-event and there is a suspicion that the ECB (European \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is smoothing the tops and bottoms to stop volatility," Stoneham said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Swiss franc continued to gain support from its safe-haven halo, Knuthsen said. "Bond markets and the Swissie will always attract nervous money." \par \par \uc2  At 1137 GMT, the Swiss franc was up at 1.3774/84, but off early highs of 1.3745 per dollar, against 1.3816/26 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Euro/Swiss franc was firm at 1.5972/77 versus 1.6009/16 in late Europe on Friday. During the session so far euro/Swiss franc has been quoted at a low of 1.59. \par \par \uc2  ((Pratima Desai, London newsroom +44 171 542 2737, pratima.desai@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1p05zyi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
