\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dlr boxed in,eyes on emerging markets. \b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 1999\par 11:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 25 (Reuters) - The dollar was confined in narrow ranges below 115 yen and around 1.16 per euro in early Monday trade as attention remained on emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is range-bound, as it was last week. Focus remains on emerging markets and their equity markets," said Kirit Shah, chief strategist at Sanwa International. \par \par \uc2  Although, a Chinese newspaper report, published Sunday, saying that a yuan devaluation may not be a bad thing was providing support for dollar/yen, Shah said. \par \par \uc2  A Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said the report was a private opinion and did not reflect official policy. \par \par \uc2  At 0835 GMT the dollar was at 114.50/60 yen, little changed from 114.65/75 in late European trade on Friday. The euro was circling $1.1600 from $1.1585/90 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  "If dollar/yen breaks higher, above 115, then it could go to 118," Shah said, who believes that the main focus in emerging markets are equity and bond markets as opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng index was down 2.45 percent at 9499.50, while the Nikkei average managed to retain some composure and ticked up 0.38 percent to 14208.81. \par \par \uc2  Focus this afternoon will be on Brazil, although a partial market holiday there may subdue trading, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Last week's proposal by Argentina to adopt the dollar as its national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may prompt speculation that other countries in emerging markets could go the same way, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "This could potentially raise dollar demand," said Shah. \par \par \uc2  Any test of the downside for the dollar, will probably stop around last week's lows near 112, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Below that, 110 will be a major barrier, created by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  a couple of weeks ago with apparent dollar-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The worries about BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are there," said Michael Derks, senior market strategist at Nomura International \par \par \uc2  "Dollar/yen is benefiting from Asian worries and these have been heightened by concerns about Brazil. Although fairly aggressive selling by Japanese exporters around 115 yen is likely to cap the upside." \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, fell to all-time lows last week, after a decision by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to let it float with the minimum of interference. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile the main beneficiary of all the emerging market uncertainty was the safe-haven Swiss franc, Derks said. \par \par \uc2  "It (Swiss franc) has had a fairly decent boost on emerging market worries over the last few trading sessions." \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc was up nearly half a percent against the dollar and euro. By 0900 GMT dollar/Swiss franc was at 1.3754/64 from 1.3816/26 in late European trade on Friday, Euro/Swiss franc was at 1.5953/58 versus 1.6009/16. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Iraq may also reemerge as an issue. \par \par \uc2  Arab newspapers said on Monday Iraq has damaged its chances of winning Middle East support after walking out of an Arab foreign ministers meeting in in Cairo on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Iraqi verbal attacks on Saudi Arabia and Kuwait were also cited as reasons for loss of support. The Arab league has called for Iraq to comply with U.S. resolutions on weapons inspections. \par \par \uc2  In terms of economic data, a 14.40 percent year-on-year rise in Japan's December custom-cleared trade surplus had little \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact, but it did underline growing U.S.-Japan trade tensions. Thr December trade surplus with the United States was up 23.0 percent from 12 months ago. \par \par \uc2  German December annualised M3 money supply grew 5.9 percent from 5.3 percent in November. \par \par \uc2  Markets are also keeping an eye on the continuation of U.S. President Bill Clinton's impeachment trial, in case of any unexpected developments, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  ((Pratima Desai, London newsroom +44 171 542 2737, pratima.desai@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1p05z8v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
