\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr steady in late Tokyo,Sakakibara remark ignored. \b0\par\par\uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1999\par 08:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO Jan 22 (Reuters) - The dollar was steady against the yen in late Friday afternoon trade in Tokyo, as the market searched in vain for fresh clues to push the dollar out of its recent 112-115 yen range. \par \par \uc2  The market was unspired by comments on Friday afternoon by Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara, who said that the Japanese government has not expressed any views on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  "All I have said is that an excessively weak yen is not desirable - as well as an excessively strong yen," Sakakibara told foreign journalists and others following a speech. \par \par \uc2  A senior dealer at a Japanese bank said: "With those remarks, Mr Sakakibara may have wanted to imply that he does not want the yen stronger than 110 (to the dollar)." \par \par \uc2  Early last week, the dollar dipped as low as 108.21 yen before apparent dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted it back above 112 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 113.65/75 yen at 0606 GMT, compared with 113.60/65 yen in New York late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara also said that Japan had no immediate intention to shift its portfolio of foreign reserves as a result of the recent introduction of the euro. \par \par \uc2  Sakakibara said the question of what type of exchange rate regime was needed was a very important one, but that he was "not necessarily suggesting that Japan would support" a target zone or reference zone regime. \par \par \uc2  "The market recently tested the dollar's resistance at 115 yen twice, but failed to push it decisively above that level," said a dealer at a city bank. \par \par \uc2  "But attempts to the push the dollar down also failed overnight due to rumours of a devaluation of the Chinese yuan. As a result, the market has lost a sense of clear direction," he added. \par \par \uc2  Rumours of a yuan devaluation briefly pushed the dollar up as high as around 114.49 yen in New York overnight. \par \par \uc2  Such talk increased after Brazil's devaluation last week, but many operators doubted that Beijing would actually follow suit. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Wednesday it was "absolutely essential" to preserve the stability of the yuan. \par \par \uc2  A Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  official in New York said on Thursday that Hong Kong remains committed to the peg between its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. dollar, despite last week's devaluation of the Brazilian real. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the dollar rose as high as 114.12 yen before slipping back below 114 yen on selling by U.S. fund operators. There was also talk of options-related dollar selling. \par \par \uc2  The market could test 115 yen again next week, but the dollar was likely to meet heavy selling from Japanese exporters and investors above 115 yen, said a dealer at a trust bank. \par \par \uc2  Worries over U.S.-Japan trade rows were also making operators cautious about buying dollars in the near term. \par \par \uc2  The Commerce Department said on Thursday that the U.S. trade deficit climbed to $15.49 billion in November from a revised $13.59 billion in October. \par \par \uc2  Asked about a rise in the U.S. trade deficit in November, Japan's Finance Minister Miyazawa said on Friday the deficit with Japan and China was the main reason behind the rise, adding: "It is likely to become a problem in the future." \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, U.S. Trade Representative Charlene Barshefsky reiterated that Japan must reduce its steel exports substantially or the United States would retaliate. \par \par \uc2  The dollar/yen rate was unmoved by minutes of the Bank of Japan's November 27 Policy Board meeting, which showed that the board voted 7-2 to keep monetary policy unchanged. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1606/14 at 0705 GMT, compared with $1.1603/08 in New York late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Short-covering interest in the euro against the yen indirectly helped to support the euro/dollar rate, dealers said. ((Kanta Watanabe, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-9780 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1m0649c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
