\par\par \b \uc2  Yen seen maintaining its stranglehold on dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Astrid Zweynert \par \uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 1999\par 14:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 21 (Reuters) - The dollar's trade-tension woes provided the gravity-defying yen with an extra fillip on Thursday, adding to a growing list of factors expected to boost the yen in the first quarter, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Yen strength is far from over," said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Markets. "I remain bearish in the longer term but the yen could trade as low as 105 in the next months." \par \par \uc2  The yen rose against the dollar after U.S. Trade Representative Charlene Barshevsky said on Wednesday the United States would act against Japan unless its steel exports in December show a substantial decline. \par \par \uc2  The yen has defied economic textbook logic by strengthening in recent weeks despite Japan's economic doldrums and market scepticism that several stimulus packages will succeed in rebooting the world's second largest economy. \par \par \uc2  A seasonal factor, the so-called repatriation of Japanese funds ahead of the fiscal year-end in late March, will continue to boost the yen in the near term, anlaysts said. \par \par \uc2  In recent years, Japanese institutions have kicked off repatriation in January, or even December, to avoid getting caught in a general rush for yen assets closer to March 31, research by HSBC shows. \par \par \uc2  The scale of capital repatriation may be increased by the low level of the Nikkei, which will force Japanese institutions to beef up the value of their domestic assets. \par \par \uc2  Despite a close above 14,000 on Thursday for the first time in over a month, the Nikkei is still over 2,000 points below its closing level of March 1998. \par \par \uc2  The yen has also been buoyed by a dramatic rise in Japanese government bond (JGB) yields since September, which is set to continue amid a JGB issuance bonanza to fill holes in Japan's budget for the next fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Even though returns on Japanese assets remain at historical lows, investing abroad has become less attractive for domestic investors since benchmark 10-year JGB yields have tripled since their September lows below 0.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  A substantial fall in the Japan premium, the extra payment battered Japanese banks have to make to raise funds in foreign interbank markets, is also positive for the yen. The three-month premium has halved since peaking in early November. \par \par \uc2  "This is a signal that Japanese banks no longer aggressively need dollars for year-end funding," said HSBC's Bloom. \par \par \uc2  Concern about trade tensions with Japan could exacerbate the dollar's woes, helping the yen by adding to dollar-negative factors such as a record current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  "The yen will probably go higher before it falls back - as the dollar is also under pressure because of ...a host of negative factors," said Claudio Piron, treasury economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  But a sharply rising yen is not welcome in Tokyo as a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hamper economic recovery by making Japanese exports more expensive. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) put its foot down last week, reportedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to halt the yen's rise. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped to put a floor under dollar/yen at around 110 yen, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ has put a firm base under dollar/yen, and for the time being the market is respecting that," said a trader at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But the yen will strengthen gradually as repatriation goes on, and maybe the BOJ can live with a gradual move higher, followed by weaker levels in the new fiscal year." \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami on Thursday highlighted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's concern about the speed of yen movements. He said the BOJ was ready to sell or buy yen when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is too volatile. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1l069lq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
