\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dlr in tight ranges, awaits Greenspan. \b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 1999\par 13:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 20 (Reuters) - The dollar was volatile but clinging to narrow ranges versus the yen and euro in midsession Europe on Wednesday before a key speech on the U.S. economy by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has had a firmer bias this week, but it has been in a range for most of this morning," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "Greenspan speaking this afternoon is more relavant than (U.S. President Bill) Clinton's speech yesterday, which had very little impact. People are looking for clues on Brazil and emerging market contagion." \par \par \uc2  At 1110 GMT, the dollar was at 113.56/69 from 113.69/74 in late European trade on Tuesday and during the session it has ranged between between 113.03 and 113.71. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at $1.1587/90 from $1.1613/19 late on Tuesday, having spent most of the session so far wedged between $1.1612 and $1.1557. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday Clinton, in his State of the Union address to Congress proposed to invest up to $700 billion of social security funds in the stock market and subsidise retirement accounts. He warned Japan to halt steel imports into the U.S. or face sanctions. \par \par \uc2  As regards the Brazilian crisis, some analysts are surprised at the subdued dollar reaction, especially given demands by state governors to renegotiate debt repayments and their continuous delays to austerity and reform measures. \par \par \uc2  "The mood in the market appears to be that the Brazilian crisis is over, we think its has worsened," said Arnaud Mares, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "The cost of servcing debt has become prohibitive, creating a bigger risk of default. There is a bit too much complacency in the market as regards Brazil." Late Monday Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the prime lending rate to 41 percent from 29 percent. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's Lower House of Congress is due to vote later Wednesday on a delayed motion that could accelerate the passage of a key pension contribution bill, regarded as an essential part of the government's austerity package. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil and the U.S. current account deficit will eventually prove to be great weights on the dollar," Mares said. \par \par \uc2  "Within weeks dollar/yen could be below the 110 level, ultimately in the Spring I see it sub-100. The Japanese as major buyers of U.S. treasury bonds are not as keen (to buy)." \par \par \uc2  But for the time being, many analysts believe a floor has been placed under dollar/yen by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BoJ last week seems to have set a 110 base for dollar/yen in the very near-term," Foley said. \par \par \uc2  "Overnight the yen was supported by higher Japanese equity prices after news that the government was considering pumping in another 20 trillion yen into real estate," Foley said. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei Average was up 1.87 percent at the close of trade on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, analysts expect a generally firmer dollar due to growing expectations of a euro zone rate cut. The first monthly report published Tuesday by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not rule out an interest rate cut, Foley said. \par \par \uc2  The ECB's governing council meets Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Hans Tietmeyer on Wednesday tried to ward off political pressure for European interest rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  He said volatile short-term oriented monetary policies were dangerous and that artificially low interest rates could lead to investment mistakes and asset price bubbles. \par \par \uc2  Tietmeyer also criticised proposals by senior euro zone officials for exchange rate systems, saying better transparency and supervision were the keys to stability. \par \par \uc2  "(U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert) Rubin is unlikely to agree to any sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls," Foley said. \par \par \uc2  ((Pratima Desai, London newsroom +44 171 542 2737, pratima.desai@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1k066s8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
