\par\par \b \uc2  Japan's Current-Account Surplus Tumbled 5.3% In November \b0\par\par\uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 1999\par 04:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's broadest measure of trade fell in November, declining 5.3% from a year ago, the government said Monday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that Japan's unadjusted current-account surplus for November fell 5.3% from the year-earlier month to 1.208 trillion yen ($10.61 billion), as the decline ended six straight months of monthly year-on-year increases in the surplus. \par \par \uc2  The merchandise-trade surplus plunged 13.4% to 1.073 trillion yen. The surplus in trade and services plummeted 25% to 654.4 billion yen, as the nation's worst recession since World War II continued to stifle demand for imports. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo financial markets didn't react to the release of the data, as the figures were generally in line with expectations. Tokyo-based economists surveyed by Dow Jones on average had forecast a surplus of 1.109 trillion yen in the current-account balance, which is the difference between the nation's income from foreign sources and foreign obligations payable excluding net capital investment. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the period of sharp growth in Japan's current-account surplus has come to an end, as a weak demand environment both at home and abroad is likely to ensure that the nation's surplus hovers around the same levels as last year. \par \par \uc2  "Depending on the pace of declines in exports and imports, the trade surplus may alternately show ups and downs this year. But the absolute levels of the trade and current account surpluses will remain flat with last year," said Mamoru Yamazaki, analyst at Paribas Capital Markets in Tokyo. "Both exports and imports remained sluggish in November, and this environment will likely continue this year," Yamazaki added. \par \par \uc2  "While domestic demand is unlikely to pick up anytime soon, exports also are likely to remain sluggish due to weak Asian demand this year," said an analyst at another foreign-securities company. \par \par \uc2  "Although the continued yen fall last year boosted Japan's trade surplus, the yen has rebounded since then. So a sustained rise in trade and current account surpluses is unlikely this year," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan has come under increasing pressure to revive its sluggish economy to import more goods and cap the surplus. Japan's economy is still struggling to shake off the effects of a plunge in land prices in the early 1990s, which buried the country's banks under a mountain of failed or risky loans. \par \par \uc2  After the dollar soared to an eight-year high of 147.61 yen in August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell back sharply in October and has remained in a weakening trend ever since. A week ago, the dollar fell to a 28-month low of 108.21 yen, but later rebounded after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling yen for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Early Monday morning, the dollar was trading around 113.40 yen. A weaker dollar makes Japanese exporters less price-competitive, and generally helps to trim Japan's trade surplus. \par \par \uc2  Notions that the strengthening of the yen will undermine Japanese exports in coming months helped weigh on stock prices last week, as stock market players sold shares of automobile makers and other leading exporters, such as Sony Corp. \par \par \uc2  But the November current-account data may not be totally bad news for Japan, as the figures could help ease trade friction with the U.S. Many economists have forecast lately that Japan's current-account surplus could continue to rise as a percentage of gross domestic product, or GDP, stoking protectionist sentiment in the U.S. GDP is the total market value of goods and services produced in the country. \par \par \uc2  Richard Jerram, chief economist at ING Barings Securities (Japan) Ltd., forecasts that Japan's current-account surplus could rise to 3.7% in the fiscal year from April, up sharply from 2.6% in the year ended last March. \par \par \uc2  Japan's refusal to open up its fisheries and forestry industries at the Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation forum last November drew sharp criticism from the U.S. And earlier this month, the U.S. government said it would closely monitor monthly movements in Japanese steel exports to the country, which doubled in 1998. If steel exports don't fall, the U.S. said it will go to the World Trade Organization or impose anti-dumping penalties under U.S. Section 201 laws. \par \par \uc2  Although Japan hasn't made any commitments, trade minister Kaoru Yosano told his U.S. counterparts Charlene Barshefsky last week that the Japanese industry projects that steel exports to the U.S. are likely to drop to levels around where they were in 1997, before the Asian crisis sharply boosted such exports in 1998. \par \par \uc2  "Possible trade friction is one risk scenario for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," Yamazaki said. But he added that the yen is unlikely to post any sharp jumps because U.S. economic growth is likely to slow at a gradual pace. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv1i01ppw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
