\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr recovers in Tokyo but still nervous on Brazil. \b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 1999\par 08:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 15 (Reuters) - The dollar firmed slightly against the yen and the euro by late Tokyo on Thursday as Asian markets appeared to be reacting relatively calmly to Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But markets in Asia were still ascertaining how Wall Street and other markets would price in the impact of the devaluation later on Thursday, which made operators reluctant about boosting their dollar positions actively by late afternoon, they said. \par \par \uc2  As of 0633 GMT, the dollar stood at 113.22/32 yen compared with 113.15/25 in late New York on Wednesday. The euro was at $1.1639/48 against $1.1666/71 in New York. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar wasn't put under severe attack because many players had already expected to see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation by Brazil. So at least in the near term, short-covering in dollar/yen should intensify," a commercial bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "It's still hard to determine the trend of dollar/yen in the longer term, while the market seems to be turning somewhat careful about selling dollars aggressively after the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (on Tuesday)," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  widened its real-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  band to allow the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade at between 1.2 and 1.32 reais from 1.22 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Although Asian share markets suffered losses after the devaluation, some dealers said the losses were limited. The reaction in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also limited, they said. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong stocks erased some of their early losses by late afternoon trade after tumbling at the opening as Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation brought added concerns to a market already weakened by worries about the debt of Chinese firms. \par \par \uc2  As of 0702 GMT, the blue-chip Hang Seng Index was down 0.91 percent at 10,179.81 points after opening down 2.10 percent at 10,058.16 points. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks took a big hit overnight, with the Dow Jones Industrial average dipping as much as 262 points before easing by the close. It ended down 125.12 points at 9,349.56. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Tokyo stocks ended sharply higher, as worries over Brazil's financial crisis were overridden by the yen's weakness against the dollar. At the close, the benchmark Nikkei average was up 335.26 points or 2.50 percent at 13,738.86. \par \par \uc2  The dollar maintained a stronger bias against the yen throughout the day amid strong short-covering demand, particularly below 113 yen, following a recent downtrend, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Early rounds of commercial demand ahead of a three-day holiday in Japan and weakness in Japan's banking shares also underpinned the greenback, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the market lacked energy to drive up the dollar decisively above 114 yen amid offers by exporters and institutional investors and uncertainty about the situation in Brazil and Latin America, they said. \par \par \uc2  Economic Planning Agency chief Taichi Sakaiya said on Thursday that the dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  level was now in an "acceptable range" for most Japanese firms after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Sakaiya's remarks blunted the dollar's upside," a dealer at a trust bank said. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the euro eased against the dollar and the yen on profit-taking following sharp overnight rallies. \par \par \uc2  "Some bought the euro for safe-haven purposes after the devaluation, but later unwound some positions," a U.S. bank dealer said. "The market now is a bit cautious about selling the euro too aggressively against the yen after the sharp rebound." \par \par \uc2  As of 0735 GMT, the euro stood at 131.94/132.14 yen against 132.09 yen in late New York trade on Wednesday. The euro fell as low as around 124.50/60 yen in overseas markets on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The markets shrugged off news that Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party (LDP) and small Liberal Party had agreed on Wednesday to form a coalition. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will closely watch the results of the two-day Asia-Europe Meeting (ASEM) that begins in Frankfurt on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Deputy finance ministers of the G7 will also meet in Frankfurt on Saturday. ((Chikafumi Hodo, Tokyo Treasury Desk +81-3 3432-8785 tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1e06m12\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
