\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dollar hit by Brazil woes, eyes Dow. \b0\par\par\uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1999\par 14:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The dollar dropped by more than one percent against the euro by Europe's midsession on Wednesday, with sentiment soured by Brazil's financial problems and the possible fallout on the U.S. economy and stock market. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty over Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy heightened as the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gustavo Franco announced his resignation and said he recognised the need for more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate policies. \par \par \uc2  Franco, one of the key defenders of Brazil's stabilisation plan, also said President Fernando Henrique Cardoso wanted a "reorientation" of these policies. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it was removing its real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's mini-band, which guides the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a daily basis, to let the market trade in the larger maxi-band. Earlier, it had re-set this maxi-band at between 1.20 and 1.32 reais per dollar compared with the previous band of 1.12 to 1.22. \par \par \uc2  Amid the Brazilian news, trade in the dollar was choppy as the market had been rife with rumours for hours about what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might announce today. \par \par \uc2  "The market had been expecting bad news from this news conference, and the dollar had been sold off against the euro and sterling beforehand," said Jesper Dannesboe, treasury economist at ABN Amro in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at $1.1723/30 per euro as of 1218 GMT, just off its session low of $1.1728. \par \par \uc2  It also lost ground against the Swiss franc, the market's safe haven when it senses the risk of widespread political or economic turmoil. Dollar/Swiss was last at a one-week low of 1.3590/00 francs, losing two percent from its session highs. \par \par \uc2  Traders in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were bracing for the opening of the Brazilian and U.S. stock markets, with traders fearing a big selloff in the Dow Jones Industrial Average after it reached a record high earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2  "There are worries that a Brazilian devaluation is coming through - with the new band set at 1.32, the market is driving the real right to that level," said Deborah Read, economist at BankAmerica in London. "But the next development is the opening of the Brazilian stock market and the fallout in the U.S." \par \par \uc2  There was some relief that the Brazilian measures were not as drastic as some had feared, but analysts said today's news still boded ill for Brazil and in effect the United States, with its strong economic links to Latin America. \par \par \uc2  "The news was not devastating, considering that some people had anticipated an Armageddon," said Dannesboe at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  "But the dollar is still a sell under the circumstances... Brazil's fiscal situation hasn't improved at all. And it was bad news in that he (Franco) had to admit the situation is so bad they couldn't stick to the old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime," he added. \par \par \uc2  Fears about Brazil's fiscal austerity plans escalated this week as the nation's third-richest state, Minas Gerais, declared a 90-day moratorium on its debt to the federal government. There is concern the standoff could derail the fiscal programme, endangering an International Monetary Fund package for Brazil. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also having a whippy session against the yen, with its moves mostly in line with those of other dollar pairs. \par \par \uc2  While the Brazilian situation dominated the market with all the signs pointing to a dollar drop across the board, traders were loath to push dollar/yen down too far just one day after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was last at 112.03/10 yen, off its session low of 111.12 which was reached before the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcements, and up by 3.5 percent from the 28-month low of 108.17 it reached on Monday. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, chizu.nomiyama@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1d06qx4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
