\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE-Dollar/yen struggles anew, euro firm. \b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1999\par 10:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The dollar was giving up its hard-won gains against the yen on Wednesday, with the market focusing on dollar-bearish factors again after the shock of Tuesday's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  began to wear off. \par \par \uc2  But fear of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepping in to buy dollars again was making yen bulls wary, particularly as U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin said on Tuesday that Washington's strong-dollar policy had not changed. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 111.56/66 yen at 0815 GMT, near the bottom of its overnight range of one yen and down from Tuesday's European closing levels of 112.03/13. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar fails to extend the highs it made yesterday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and after the Treasury Secretary reiterated his strong dollar policy, it will show there is still very good selling interest in the dollar," said Daniel Katzive, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market strategist at Standard & Poor's MMS in London. \par \par \uc2  "Long-term players might not want to miss the opportunity to sell if they think dollar/yen's going to go below 110 again." \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen bounced to 112.80 on Tuesday, coinciding with Wednesday's high, on the back of the apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which dealers said totalled more than $1 billion, although Japanese authorities have not confirmed or commented on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tokyo officials were keeping the market on its toes, however, with Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa saying on Wednesday that there was still a need to carefully monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves for a while. \par \par \uc2  But the market was firmly eyeing bearish factors for the U.S. economy. At the top of the list was concern about progress in Brazil's fiscal plans, the flight of capital out of that country and the potential impact on the U.S. stock market. \par \par \uc2  "The Brazil situation is assuming more importance for everyone and after the outflow we saw yesterday, it is becoming the top concern," said Stephen Lewis, chief economist at Monument Derivatives. \par \par \uc2  "And so the dollar is being weighed down because it is the reference \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Latin America, which could be facing more financial problems," Lewis added. \par \par \uc2  Brazil lost a net $500 million through \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Tuesday on mounting concern that the country's austerity plans could be undermined by Brazilian state Minas Gerais' declaration last week of a moratorium on debt payments to the central government. \par \par \uc2  Such worries about the biggest economy in Latin America - a crucial trading partner for the United States - sparked a sell-off in the U.S. stock market on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Heightening trade tensions between the United States and Japan were also dampening dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Trade Representative Charlene Barshefsky, speaking on Tuesday after a meeting with Japan's trade minister Kaoru Yosano, said Washington would not tolerate any unfair trade practices and that bilateral tensions had increased "dramatically". \par \par \uc2  Signs of U.S-Japan hostility on trade issues tend to push dollar/yen lower due to fears that Washington will call for a weaker dollar/yen to make its exports more competitive in Japan. \par \par \uc2  The euro was stronger against a generally softer dollar, with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  notching lower as fears about the Brazilian situation escalated on Brazilian media reports that the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor had asked to resign. \par \par \uc2  Euro/dollar was quoted at $1.1646/52, up from $1.1554/63 seen in late European trade on Tuesday. It was also up by about two percent from a low of $1.1440 seen on Tuesday, which was the lowest since its market debut on January 4. \par \par \uc2  ((Chizu Nomiyama, London newsroom 44 171 542 6721, chizu.nomiyama@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1d06pyy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
