\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  seen at $2 bln, more may come. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1999\par 09:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan (BOJ) likely bought around $2 billion or more against the yen in the Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday, bankers said on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action on Tuesday was its first since it conducted a number of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1996, while the last significant dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in August 1995, when it acted in concert with the U.S. authorities. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to support the dollar on Wednesday and after that if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls towards 110 yen amid a lack of bullish factors to aid the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 112.43/48 yen at 0228 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ's dollar buying was persistent throughout Tuesday and the total would be around $2 billion or even more," said a senior trader at a major Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  Other bankers said the BOJ's dollar purchases could be in excess of $2 billion. \par \par \uc2  According to bankers, the Tokyo market is "a bit long on the dollar" as a result of Tuesday's BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would blunt the impact of possible dollar buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the week. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said, however, that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may still be willing to prop up the dollar against the yen to help defend Japan's fragile economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "Except for the BOJ, there is no bullish factor for the dollar in the market, amid lingering market concerns over the Brazil crisis and its implications for the future U.S. economy and markets," said a senior European bank trader. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated nearly $1 billion in capital flowed out of Brazil on Tuesday, the highest daily loss since the Latin American powerhouse narrowly avoided a devastating devaluation last year, and a sign that a new crisis is deepening fast. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said concerns over Brazil's chances of surviving Asia-style financial collapse were quickly turning to panic. \par \par \uc2  The United States has some $35 billion in private investment in Brazil. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, however, authorities are more concerned about the weak dollar's impact on Japan's markets and its economy. \par \par \uc2  A strong yen has been a major factor behind renewed weakness in the Tokyo stock market, including selloffs not only of exporters' shares but also those of banking firms. \par \par \uc2  This in turn undermines recent government attempts to jump-start Japan's economy, now in its worst recession since World War Two, through aggressive fiscal stimulus steps. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were aimed at halting a further assault on the dollar, which had taken a 7.5 percent dive against the yen in just two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the apparent dollar buying by the BOJ pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up more than four yen to 112.85 yen in Tokyo on Tuesday from its early traded low of 108.65 yen. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's estimated purchase amount on Tuesday compared with its record monthly dollar buying of $15.3 billion in February 1996, when the dollar was around 103-106 yen. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the possibility of the U.S. monetary authorities joining the BOJ in such dollar-buying moves was slim. \par \par \uc2  The last U.S.-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  joint dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on August 2, 1995, when the greenback took a dive to below 90 yen. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, U.S. Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin reiterated his preference for a strong dollar, which he said had underpinned the still-solid U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  "In terms of our dollar policy, I think the strong dollar has served us well over the past six years. It has contributed to lower interest rates and lower inflation and greater confidence in this country, and that policy of a strong dollar remains unchanged," he told reporters in a briefing. \par \par \uc2  But bankers said Rubin's verbal support for a strong dollar did not signal any intention by the United States to spend money to protect its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  ((-Tokyo Treasury Desk (813) 3432-8676 \par \par \uc2  tokyo.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1d06py6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
