\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  IN EUROPE - Dollar in thrall of Brazil concern. \b0\par\par\uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1999\par 17:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The dollar was buffeted on Wednesday by concern that renewed economic and financial turmoil in Brazil would hurt America's economy and stock market. \par \par \uc2  It fell as much as 3.4 percent against the Swiss franc and 2.0 percent against the euro after Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, was effectively devalued and the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, Gustavo Franco, resigned. \par \par \uc2  Expectations mounted that Brazil, the world's eight biggest economy and a major U.S. trading partner, would have to keep interest rates high to the detriment of growth as the real quickly hit its newly designated floor. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded from its lows later in Europe, led by its rally to one-and-a-half-week highs against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Traders cited wariness of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and speculation Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be next in line to suffer as traders bailed out of emerging markets. Still, analysts said the dollar's resilience could prove short-lived. \par \par \uc2  "The Brazil situation is as bad as it seems and market forces are likely take it to its logical conclusion which is a full-blown speculative attack," said Stewart Newnham, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The fundamentals are therefore for a weaker dollar." \par \par \uc2  The dollar fell to one-week lows of 1.3405 Swiss francs and $1.1788 per euro during the European session but had trimmed its losses by 1602 GMT to 1.3530/40 and $1.1700/05. That was still weaker than Tuesday's late European close of 1.3873/83 and $1.1554/63. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was 113.73/78, its highest since January 4, and even stronger than the 112.80 peak hit on Tuesday after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was reported to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the dollar would remain well supported against the yen in the near term given traders were still smarting from the sharp reversal of yen gains triggered by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was an important signal to the market and has proved to be very effective," said Gerard Lyons, economist at DKB in London. "The market feels it has little to gain by pushing dollar/yen lower." \par \par \uc2  While others were less sanguine about the dollar's outlook, bears were kept at bay for the time being as the U.S. Dow Jones Industrial Average trimmed initial losses of 2.7 percent and Brazil's Bovespa's stock index halved its early morning drop. \par \par \uc2  Concern about Brazil's situation was also kept in check for now as U.S. President Bill Clinton said his administration was working hard to help the country overcome its troubles. \par \par \uc2  In Europe, sterling was another beneficiary of the dollar's weakness, rising as high as $1.6592. While it eased back to $1.6488/98 by 1636 GMT, it is no longer seen at risk of sliding towards the $1.6080 lows traders were targeting as recently as yesterday. \par \par \uc2  One of the few \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to benefit from the dollar's weakness on Wednesday was the Australian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Concern about weak commodity prices drove the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as low as $0.6280. That compares with overnight highs around $0.6370/75 and a one-and-a-half month peak of $0.6440 reached on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Commodities Research Bureau index was last down 1.31 percent. ((Swaha Pattanaik, London newsroom, +44 171 542 6284, swaha.pattanaik@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  . \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1d06jvz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
