\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2 /Yen-3:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Industry Worried By Strong Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 335 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1999\par 11:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Adding to the tension were increasing signs of discontent among Japan's business leaders, who had begun lobbying the government to support the dollar. Jiro Ushio, head of the Japan Association of Corporate Executives, urged the government last week to declare its opposition to the rising yen. "There's been a communication gap. We should declare that we don't welcome the stronger yen for the next five to six months," he said, adding that in the medium-term, a strong yen might not be so problematic. \par \par \uc2  Hiroyuki Yoshino, president of Honda Motor Co., said Tuesday that given the state of the economy, the yen is too high, while Katsuhiko Machida, president of Sharp Corp., said that the company's sales would be affected by about Y1 billion with every Y1 fluctuation against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Until Tuesday, Japanese monetary officials had kept mum, generally declining to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As recently as last week, with the dollar around Y113, Vice Finance Minister Eisuke Sakakibara offered tacit approval for a strong yen, saying, "Should the Japanese economy pick up from the middle of the year, the base trend (in 1999) will be a stronger yen." \par \par \uc2  Tuesday morning, however, Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa changed the tenor of the ministry's stance, warning market participants, "I am watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely." \par \par \uc2  Later in the day, Chief Cabinet Spokesman Hiromu Nonaka said at a news conference, "Neither excessive yen appreciation nor depreciation is something we want from the standpoint of the world economy." \par \par \uc2  The decision to put a stop to the dollar's decline is also consistent with comments made lately by Japanese officials who said stability among the the dollar, euro and yen should be a priority for policy makers. The move also falls in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stated objective of maintaining a loose monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  -By Brian Fowler (813-5255-2929;bfowler@ap.org) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 12-01-99 \par \par \uc2  1022GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv1c008cp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
